true] [17592186231290 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231292 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231293 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231294 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1075 #edb/ref 17592186231295 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1077 "selection" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231290 1080 #uuid "31d927fa-1e05-525d-99d4-49a294db3d0b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231291 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231291 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231291 1084 #uuid "8fc1c5c6-17d3-5176-a04e-2ebd06c355f1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231292 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231292 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231292 1084 #uuid "931398ff-b392-5441-a1b9-5186bc5e7885" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231293 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231293 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231293 1084 #uuid "eae9b432-8ee4-511f-927a-8105253ad4d1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231294 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231294 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231294 1084 #uuid "01a4a592-3793-5cc8-9bf8-3e69abc4badf" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231295 1081 "I only" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231295 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231295 1084 #uuid "68fb3c66-8eb8-5c4c-aafb-0f429a8e9cf7" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231296 1069 "e-21850" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231296 1071 "$10x^2y'' + 7xy' + (3x-1)y = 0$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n)$, given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n$a_n = -\\dfrac{3a_{n-1}}{f(n)}, \\qquad n \\geq 1$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$10x^{2}y^{″}|= 10(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)10((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 7xy^{′}|= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n},; (3x - 1)y|=-a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(3a_{n - 1} - a_{n})x^{λ + n}$.*" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231296 1072 #uuid "aea13ce3-4614-55ab-ae03-0088b5e3c447" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231296 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}n=0\n$$\n\nterm isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x^{2}y^{″} &= 10(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)10((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n7xy^{′} &= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n(3x - 1)y &= -a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(3a_{n - 1} - a_{n})x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + 7λa_{0}x^{λ} - a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(10λ^{2} - 10λ + 7λ - 1)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= (10λ^{2} - 3λ - 1)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[10((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n} + 7(λ + n)a_{n} + (3a_{n - 1} - a_{n})]x^{λ + n} &=  \\\\\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[10(λ + n)^{2}a_{n} - 3(λ + n)a_{n} - a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1}]x^{λ + n} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(10(λ + n)^{2} - 3(λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1}]x^{n} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10λ^{2} - 3λ - 1)a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(10(λ + n)^{2} - 3(λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(10λ^{2} - 3λ - 1)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(10(λ + n)^{2} - 3(λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10λ^{2} - 3λ - 1)a_{0} &= 0 \\\\\n10λ^{2} - 3λ - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2λ - 1)(5λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= -\\frac{1}{5}, \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$, we take\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nequal to $0$, we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10(λ + n)^{2} - 3(λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(2(λ + n) - 1)(5(λ + n) + 1)a_{n} + 3a_{n - 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2(\\frac{1}{2} + n) - 1)(5(\\frac{1}{2} + n) + 1)a_{n} &= -3a_{n - 1} \\\\\n2n(5n + \\frac{7}{2})a_{n} &= -3a_{n - 1} \\\\\nn(10n + 7)a_{n} &= -3a_{n - 1} \\\\\na_{n} &= - (3a_{n - 1})/(n(10n + 7))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=n(10n+7)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1069 "e-21859" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n$y'-6y = f(x), \\qquad y(1)=4$\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p(1)=0$.*\n\n$AAA?AAA$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1072 #uuid "b7a81b9e-d12c-55c7-b755-8f4e5b8115c7" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of $y' - 6y = 0$ is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator $y' - 6y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim Ae^{6x} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=4$. Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(1) &= Ae^{6(1)} + y_{p}(1) \\\\\n4 &= Ae^{6} + 0 \\\\\nA &= 4e^{-6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\ny_c = (4e^{-6})e^{6x} = 4e^{6x-6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{4e^{6x-6}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1069 "e-21874" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5$\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0$.*\n$\\frac{1}{xxx}?\\frac{1}{xxx}$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1072 #uuid "b71e74ab-9cb3-5e43-94aa-52c2a926dc5d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1074 "We write the solution as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\n, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y=0\n$$\n\nIts general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(0)|= 0 + B\\sqrt{2}(1) + y_{p}^{′}(0); 0|= B\\sqrt{2} + 0; B|= 0$\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)$.\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(1)|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + y_{p}^{′}(1); - 5|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + 0; A|= (5)/(\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}))$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt gray]{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2})}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1069 "e-21876" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1071 "$y''+ 7\\pi^2 y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0$\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$AAA?AAA$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1072 #uuid "97290d74-457b-522d-991d-7891e5a01d28" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution $y_p$. Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 7\\pi^2 = 0\n$$\n\ngives roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{7}\\pi \\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= A\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) + y_{p}(0) \\\\\n0 &= A(1) + 0 + 0 \\\\\nA &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $y_p(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition $y'(0)=0$. First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\ny'(x) \\sim B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi)\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n\\pi^2\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying $y'(0)=0$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= B(\\sqrt{7}π) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB(\\sqrt{7}π) &= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB &= -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}π}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\n&= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{7}nπ^{3}(28 - n^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into\n\n$$\ny_c = B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nwe find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{-\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{\\sqrt{7}n\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231300 1069 "e-21877" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231300 1071 "A particle of mass $0.25\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 3\\sin(6t)\\,\\textrm{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231300 1072 #uuid "7ad46566-b122-5de3-9b87-9beec9ac6761" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231300 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nmy'' = -ky + F(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.25y'' =-ky + 3\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 4ky = 12\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos \\omega t + B\\sin \\omega t\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega$ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4k \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac14\\omega^2\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force\n\n$$\nf(t)=12\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nwhich is $6$, is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2 = \\dfrac14\\cdot6^2 = 9 \\,\\textrm{kg/s}^2\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231301 1069 "e-21878" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231301 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t)$.\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n3. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$, and $\\phi$ are constants." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231301 1072 #uuid "f41473ff-05a6-5f3e-ae5b-0584810aa0dd" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231301 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\nThe external force is\n\n$$\nf(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nwhich is sinusoidal with frequency $4$. Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4$.\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n2. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n3. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t))\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[8(D - 2Ct)\\cos (4t) - 8(C + 2Dt)\\sin (4t)] + 16t(C\\cos (4t) + D\\sin (4t)) &= 4\\cos (4t) + 12\\sin (4t) \\\\\n8D\\cos (4t) - 8C\\sin (4t) &= 4\\cos (4t) + 12\\sin (4t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients,\n\n$$\nC=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n3. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231302 1069 "e-21887" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231302 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y''+8y=f(x)$\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. What Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_p$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231302 1072 #uuid "f4a74f28-4b3b-5602-b5e5-53c8590d4a48" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231302 1074 "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231303 1069 "e-21894" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231303 1071 "![](images/fa/fae88c8da0b50da2bc025cbac0dbc376be3b67b69b59823746873c6e7d102dcc.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231303 1072 #uuid "20c07bb4-e264-5674-a7f0-9a833f68d037" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231303 1074 "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\cos (4t)) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(30I + 500q + 5\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n- 24\\sin (4t) &= 30\\frac{dI}{dt} + 500\\frac{dq}{dt} + 5\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231304 1069 "e-21895" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231304 1071 "![](images/08/085fe02b43301629af58379289fc9d1c49c90e71a83e0dd33f491ecf4b21bfc8.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ and the initial current is $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231304 1072 #uuid "6f2eeea4-7339-5f96-b3e0-fa160a791034" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231304 1074 "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $10\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 10I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.25}=4q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=10I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(9) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(10I + 4q + 4\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n0 &= 10\\frac{dI}{dt} + 4\\frac{dq}{dt} + 4\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac52\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=0\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form\n\n$$\nI=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwe obtain two solutions, namely, $I=e^{-2t}$ and\n\n$$\nI=e^{-t/2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-t/2}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\non the capacitor and an initial current of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}\n$$\n\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac94-\\dfrac52I-q\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions\n\n$$\nI(0)=\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI^{′}(0) &= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{5}{2}I(0) - q(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{40}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ into our general solution gives\n$\\frac{3}{4}|= Ae^{-2(0)} + Be^{-0/2}; \\frac{3}{4}|= A + B$.\n- Differentiating $I$, we get\n$I'=-2Ae^{-2t}-\\dfrac12Be^{-t/2}$.\nSubstituting in $I'(0)=-\\dfrac1{40}$ gives\n$-\\frac{1}{40}|=-2Ae^{-2(0)} - \\frac{1}{2}Be^{-0/2}; - \\frac{1}{40}|=-2A - \\frac{1}{2}B$.\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{3}{4} = A + B; - \\frac{1}{40} =-2A - \\frac{1}{2}B\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\nA=-\\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB=\\dfrac{59}{60}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\dfrac{7}{30}e^{-2t}+\\dfrac{59}{60}e^{-t/2}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231305 1069 "e-21896" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231305 1071 "![](images/32/323324bc8d0ab017d30bc2a37e5e5e66784d64da6309fc131d56cb576ed2b003.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231305 1072 #uuid "cc9247b8-2a3d-5559-af6a-0cf1d6fc7a96" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231305 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 8I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\cos (2t)) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(8I + 4q + 4\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n- 12\\sin (2t) &= 8\\frac{dI}{dt} + 4\\frac{dq}{dt} + 4\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n$I_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t}$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t$, i.e.,\n$I_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute this, as well as\n$\\frac{dI_{p}}{dt}|=-2α\\sin (2t) + 2β\\cos (2t),; \\frac{d^{2}I_{p}}{dt^{2}}|=-4α\\cos (2t) - 4β\\sin (2t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-4α\\cos (2t) - 4β\\sin (2t)) + 2(-2α\\sin (2t) + 2β\\cos (2t)); + (α\\cos (2t) + β\\sin (2t))|=-3\\sin (2t); (-3α + 4β)\\cos (2t) + (-4α - 3β)\\sin (2t)|=-3\\sin (2t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-3α + 4β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (2t)) \\\\ -4α - 3β =-3\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (2t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$I_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231306 1069 "e-21903" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231306 1071 "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n$y''(t) + y(t) = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\qquad y'(0)=0$\n\nis given by\n\n$s^2Y(s) + Y(s) = \\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231306 1072 #uuid "029321f3-c767-5a77-95d7-5b4150e24f42" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231306 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s^{2} + 1)Y(s) &= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} \\\\\nY(s) &= e^{-2s} \\cdot (1)/(s(s^{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s(s^2+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s(s^2+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{s} - \\dfrac{s}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\frac{1}{s} - \\frac{s}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nf(t) = 1 - \\cos(t)\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{e^{-as}F(s)\\} = u_a(t)f(t-a)\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= L^{-1}{e^{-2s}(\\frac{1}{s} - \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 1})} \\\\\ny(t) &= u_{2}(t)(1 - \\cos (t - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t) = u(t-2)\\left(1-\\cos(t-2)\\right)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231307 1069 "e-21904" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231307 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n$y'' + 25y = 6u_2(t)\\sin(5(t-2)), \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0$,\nwhere $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231307 1072 #uuid "9941d046-be37-5ada-8d98-ecedfcb9d7f0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231307 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″} + 25y} &= L{6u_{2}(t)\\sin (5(t - 2))} \\\\\nL{y^{″}} + 25L{y} &= L{6u_{2}(t)\\sin (5(t - 2))}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y'' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'' \\right\\} = s^2Y(s) - sy(0) - y'(0) = s^2Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)f(t-a)\\} = e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(t-2) = 6\\sin(5(t-2))\n$$\n\n, so\n\n$$\nf(t) = 6\\sin(5t)\n$$\n\nThe transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{6\\sin(5t)\\} = 6 \\cdot \\frac{5}{s^2+5^2} = \\frac{30}{s^2+25}\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{6u_2(t)\\sin(5(t-2))\\} = e^{-2s} \\cdot \\frac{30}{s^2+25} = \\frac{30e^{-2s}}{s^2+25}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^{2}Y(s) + 25Y(s) &= \\frac{30e^{-2s}}{s^{2} + 25} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 25)Y(s) &= \\frac{30e^{-2s}}{s^{2} + 25} \\\\\nY(s) &= (30e^{-2s})/((s^{2} + 25)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231308 1069 "e-21907" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231308 1071 "Which of the pictures below shows a line of symmetry for the figure?\n\n![](images/e2/e2510e217444df2465824905b0215cbabc4a6963a7d71f957a23251206c79768.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231308 1072 #uuid "33a7ec9c-63ad-5d3c-bf7d-22927080f89b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231308 1074 "A line of symmetry for a figure is a line such that folding the figure along the line produces two matching halves.\n\nLet's consider each of the lines in turn.\n\n- By folding the polygon along line I, we do *not* map the polygon onto itself.![](images/cd/cdc2cb0f6edaf1b63a11299b91f010977b339d99267ac78a81937cc0cdcbd38a.png)\n\n- By folding the polygon along line II, we map the polygon onto itself.![](images/02/023790fbfaba06e3205bd726276fe7666d48aeb97c28de6c337146f2af89f013.png)\n\n- By folding the polygon along line III, we do *not* map the polygon onto itself.![](images/83/83981d2603553a0f165d7773063d049ea20ae2e3ef219f9afb189a912569d9e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231309 1069 "e-21909" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231309 1071 "How many lines of symmetry does the polygon shown below have?\n\n![](images/4b/4b8097b1f7c16c8761d47009ba5c7485a0a7db239d6523495ce72ca99e3d8ef4.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231309 1072 #uuid "461cfba3-df1b-578d-a4b8-92b909a645cc" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231309 1074 "A line of symmetry for a figure is a line such that folding the figure along the line produces two matching halves.\n\nThe polygon has $2$ lines of symmetry, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf51e5e5a9a7196de9b53264ebd0001ea4305a3c7b17a621c8a36c3d065f6d8b.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231310 1069 "e-21935" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231310 1071 "How many $\\dfrac{5}{12}\\,\\textrm{m}$ long boards can be cut from a $\\dfrac{5}{3}\\,\\textrm{m}$ plank of wood?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231310 1072 #uuid "d0ed49d8-e5f5-5960-9a94-4453af7c1aa7" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231310 1074 "To determine the number of boards, we need to divide the length of the plank by the length of one board:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac53 \\div \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac53 \\div \\dfrac{5}{12} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction’s denominator ($\\color{red}3$) divides the second fraction’s numerator ($\\color{red}12$), and the second fraction’s denominator ($\\color{blue}5$) divides the first fraction’s numerator ($\\color{blue}5$).\n\nSo, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{blue}5} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}12}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify each fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac55 = 5 \\div 5 = 1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{12}{3} = 12 \\div 3 = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac55 \\times \\dfrac{12}{3} = 1 \\times 4 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plank can be cut into $4$ boards." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231311 1069 "e-21936" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231311 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac67$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231311 1072 #uuid "3a174fd3-d181-51b5-ba02-8acb32470293" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231311 1074 "We find the length of one side of the rectangle by dividing the area by the length of the other side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7} \\div 3\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac67 \\div 3 = \\dfrac67 \\times \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac67 \\times \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac{6 \\times 1}{7 \\times 3} = \\dfrac6{21}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our result by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac6{21} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{21 \\div 3} = \\dfrac27\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231312 1069 "e-21937" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231312 1071 "A container holds $\\dfrac{5}{18}\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of clay. Each small sculpture requires $\\dfrac{2}{27}\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of clay. How many sculptures can be made with the clay in the container? Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231312 1072 #uuid "11bf1fc3-14f7-5bbd-b09d-2339cd320d85" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231312 1074 "To determine how many sculptures can be made, we need to divide the total amount of clay by the amount used for each sculpture:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{18} \\div \\dfrac{2}{27}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{27}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{27}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{18} \\div \\dfrac{2}{27} = \\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}18} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}27}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction’s denominator ($\\color{red}18$) and the second fraction’s numerator ($\\color{blue}27$) have a common factor of $9$.\n\nSo, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}27}{\\color{red}18}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times \\dfrac{27 \\div 9}{18 \\div 9} = \\dfrac{5}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, to multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nsculptures can be made." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231313 1069 "e-21938" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231313 1071 "How many $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{kg}$ bags of flour can be filled using $\\dfrac{7}{6}\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of flour? Give your answer as a **mixed number** in its simplest form." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231313 1072 #uuid "ec00d466-a190-5327-892c-fc8352604500" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231313 1074 "To determine the number of bags that can be filled, we need to divide the total amount of flour by the amount in one bag:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac76 \\div \\dfrac34\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac43\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac76 \\div \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac76 \\times \\dfrac43\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac76 \\times \\dfrac43 = \\dfrac{7 \\times 4}{6 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{28}{18}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{28}{18} = \\dfrac{14}{9}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{9} = 1\\,\\textrm{R}5 = 1\\,\\dfrac59\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac59\n$$\n\nbags of flour can be filled." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231314 1069 "e-21941" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231314 1071 "What is the $x$-coordinate of the point $T$?\n\n![](images/49/49787ecbc5e3d0e55d0b8ef116ad527dc3b3efadf3903eac94a577212340cbbd.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231314 1072 #uuid "eac5d193-35c2-532d-af06-867c4d5e2f7c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231314 1074 "To find the coordinates of $T$, we start at the origin, move $1.5$ units *right*, and then $2.5$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/20/20eabfa979adb93931624ffdc049dbf597a236c55002cdfaabd04ada96763712.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $T$ are $(1.5,-2.5)$. Therefore, the $x$-coordinate of $T$ is $1.5$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231315 1069 "e-21942" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231315 1071 "Find $\\left|-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right|$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231315 1072 #uuid "2f01203d-b3ca-576d-b6de-5a07614ac538" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231315 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo find the absolute value of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nwe find the distance between that number and zero on a number line.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa67be3e62ca36b669c782bbaf26d12e56e2bf7297be98b3dd6d5f849b81874.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the absolute value of a rational number, we can simply drop the negative sign. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{aligned} \\left|-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right &= \\dfrac{3}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231316 1069 "e-21943" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231316 1071 "What mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/f4/f4c8a9c8e3bc6354342c422f7896b89a28501ba73b0b5df934afc23792ae4977.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231316 1072 #uuid "d86b6516-f808-5def-854c-c19b9083505e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231316 1074 "The line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/34/3457b31eee6e481aaec284d605dc552c02a0dd32659b5bab5535883b7609e2d5.png)\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making $\\color{red}1$ whole step plus $\\color{blue}1$ part step (out of a possible ${\\color{blue}{3}})$.\n\n![](images/a1/a122add2976917e1d90d335d1427aaddf3bfc7162d71fa1c03b630ce64576c86.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231317 1069 "e-21944" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231317 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac23$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231317 1072 #uuid "0ed4ed33-74db-59ff-88ae-4fee2b5c8b61" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231317 1074 "We find the length of one side of the rectangle by dividing the area by the length of the other side:\n\n$$\n5 \\div \\dfrac23\n$$\n\nDividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac23\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n5 \\div \\dfrac23 = \\dfrac51 \\div \\dfrac23 = \\dfrac51 \\times \\dfrac32\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac51 \\times \\dfrac32 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 3}{1 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{15}{2}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231318 1069 "e-21945" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231318 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac16$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac56$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231318 1072 #uuid "52745771-0aba-5660-a741-3a30cfa93cc0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231318 1074 "We find the length of one side of the rectangle by dividing the area by the length of the other side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac16\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac61\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56 \\div \\dfrac16 = \\dfrac56 \\times \\dfrac61\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56 \\times \\dfrac61 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 6}{6 \\times 1} = \\dfrac{30}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{6} = 30 \\div 6 = 5\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231319 1069 "e-21946" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231319 1071 "Determine the missing side length in the area model below. Give your answer as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac49$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac65$ |\n|  |  |" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231319 1072 #uuid "7a51179d-f8c4-539c-ad3a-d56618b46d62" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231319 1074 "We find the length of one side of the rectangle by dividing the area by the length of the other side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac65 \\div \\dfrac49\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac49\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac94\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac65 \\div \\dfrac49 = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{5} \\times \\dfrac9{\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction’s numerator ($\\color{blue}6$) and the second fraction’s denominator ($\\color{red}4$) have a common factor of $2$.\n\nSo, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac95\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6} \\div 2}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 2} \\times \\dfrac95 = \\dfrac32 \\times \\dfrac95\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac32 \\times \\dfrac95 = \\dfrac{3 \\times 9}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{27}{10}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231320 1069 "e-21949" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231320 1071 "What improper fraction is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/d5/d50eb6a042d4d51443f517e4132af9dc1addd4153c2f36faa49e171d4d27d120.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231320 1072 #uuid "4cddb850-2799-5ce2-8e38-1e213fa5f09a" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231320 1074 "Notice that the line segment from $0$ to $-1$ is split into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/76/7660cd2f049d3379cace5073b9fe7df3197c3abee65cba5f03b8930fe8dac79e.png)\n\nThe number we are interested in is located\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4}+3=\\color{red}7\n$$\n\nsteps from $0$ (right to left).\n\n![](images/22/22b29ac361b2fcb3740197c065901df8b6ef3c76d587018ab99be58f3404298b.png)\n\nTherefore, since our number lies to the left of zero, the picture represents\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231321 1069 "e-21950" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231321 1071 "What mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/f0/f0ba11e8a84ca8c69f0537acb78f91e434e1c497b8dc6f84101631b6346a2f2c.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231321 1072 #uuid "fa18aee7-6e3c-5d3f-93b4-b37dbccd5c6a" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231321 1074 "The line segment from $0$ to $-1$ is split into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/91/91a61a00bc9d8e5011b7c74dcb33afa8e5cf8e5f256e0431dc06f63285fb8c26.png)\n\nStarting from zero (right to left), we land on our point by making $\\color{red}1$ whole step plus $\\color{blue}1$ part step (out of a possible ${\\color{blue}{4}})$.\n\n![](images/45/458b9a3c83c8c8500a1f47283de9342ec12bf1895c78e7c036059a019a20bda9.png)\n\nTherefore, since our number lies left of zero, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n-{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{4}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231322 1069 "e-21951" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231322 1071 "What are the coordinates of the point $P$?\n\n![](images/98/98d588dc326e3dc1a7037eafb3c21b8681d4f59ec24a43bf934b62be373db285.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231322 1072 #uuid "93aedd56-11f5-5cd4-bb49-f5fd73e79670" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231322 1074 "To find the coordinates of $P$, we start at the origin, move $2$ units *right*, and then $1.5$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/35/359b30388e1e106bd4589161a676ab4ef8c05b8c2364cf9b852dc387f70e96a0.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(2,-1.5)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231323 1069 "e-21952" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231323 1071 "What is the $y$-coordinate of the point $S$?\n\n![](images/a7/a7690023abccd40b8de4d6bf6661bfd5b1d103d5328c5b3f808958d5f170d84d.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231323 1072 #uuid "90ed4f23-1ac6-5e31-a038-3b934e41a8ff" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231323 1074 "To find the coordinates of $S$, we start at the origin, move $0.3$ units *right*, and then $0.4$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/52/52017ea1638858fb8476d348b034488bf1895d78da2d4b26407e0633ca4a738e.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $S$ are $(0.3,0.4)$. Therefore, the $y$-coordinate of $S$ is $0.4$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231324 1069 "e-21953" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231324 1071 "What are the coordinates of the point $W$?\n\n![](images/df/df29a69afefbf3ddc4af858e687547a28d959db1530407d9ecc98839359c4d61.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231324 1072 #uuid "cf320c63-2041-54cb-a7f7-6323037338a1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231324 1074 "To find the coordinates of $W$, we start at the origin, move $0.3$ units *left*, and then $0.2$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/3a/3a4a6da21452afc39aa9b60b74263f40c22e004c0dac9c1d6833326f2747134b.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $W$ are $(-0.3,0.2)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231325 1069 "e-21962" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231325 1071 "If $r=12$, then $r-9=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231325 1072 #uuid "ace5c6da-d898-545e-be49-9317100f0e02" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231325 1074 "Replacing $r$ with $12$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 12 - 9 = 3\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231326 1069 "e-21963" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231326 1071 "If $r=\\dfrac57$, then $r^2=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231326 1072 #uuid "dc812edf-4737-575a-937b-89bc2af88c38" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231326 1074 "Replacing $r$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{7})^{2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5}{7} \\cdot \\frac{5}{7} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 5}{7 \\cdot 7} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{49} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231327 1069 "e-21964" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231327 1071 "If $p=\\dfrac34$, then $4p=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231327 1072 #uuid "ea250af1-a40c-5833-89dc-2df124e0dd71" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231327 1074 "Replacing $p$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4p &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot p &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{12}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231328 1069 "e-21965" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231328 1071 "If $k = 30$, then $\\dfrac{k}5 - 3 =$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231328 1072 #uuid "e02d2e62-fedb-500d-8416-07e4a1e71e0e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231328 1074 "Replacing $k$ with $30$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{5} - 3 &=  \\\\\nk \\div 5 - 3 &=  \\\\\n30 \\div 5 - 3 &=  \\\\\n6 - 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231329 1069 "e-21966" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231329 1071 "The distance (in kilometers) that a delivery drone travels after $t$ hours is given by the expression\n$14 - 3t$.\nWhat distance does the drone travel after $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ hours?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231329 1072 #uuid "59443451-978e-5ced-80e3-8dbb5aa63fe5" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231329 1074 "To determine the distance after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours, we replace $t$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 3t &=  \\\\\n14 - 3 \\cdot t &=  \\\\\n14 - \\frac{3}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &=  \\\\\n14 - \\frac{15}{2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{28}{2} - \\frac{15}{2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{2} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance the drone travels after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{13}{2}}\n$$\n\nkilometers." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231330 1069 "e-21968" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231330 1071 "If $k=4$, then $3k^2=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231330 1072 #uuid "5027578e-0ec2-5db5-8516-7a8d063b51a0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231330 1074 "Replacing $k$ with $4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot k^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 4^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 16 &=  \\\\\n&= 48 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231331 1069 "e-21969" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231331 1071 "If $n=4$, then $3n^2 + 5=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231331 1072 #uuid "6f50924b-630b-5d74-a6ba-8becf3ce8408" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231331 1074 "Replacing $n$ with $4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3n^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot n^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 4^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 16 + 5 &=  \\\\\n48 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 53 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231332 1069 "e-21970" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231332 1071 "The amount of paint (in liters) used to cover a wall is given by the expression $\\dfrac12w^2+\\dfrac34$, where $w$ is the width of the wall in meters. How much paint is used when the width of the wall is $\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231332 1072 #uuid "8df55194-b24b-54ad-b6cb-1ec76c833210" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231332 1074 "To determine how much paint is used when the width of the wall is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe evaluate the expression at\n\n$$\nw=\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nFirst, replacing $w$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nin the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, evaluating the resulting expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{3}{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{24}{32} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{49}{32} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of paint used is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{32}\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231333 1069 "e-21973" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231333 1071 "$9x - 4x =$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231333 1072 #uuid "397919cc-d741-5cda-b965-6c580266770f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231333 1074 "We have two like terms, $9x$ and $-4x$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term by subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 4x &=  \\\\\n(9 - 4)x &=  \\\\\n&= 5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231334 1069 "e-21974" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231334 1071 "$6a - 2a + 5a + 3 =$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231334 1072 #uuid "cc542e1d-b768-5c39-9e88-1f60f3e31e6d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231334 1074 "We have three like terms, $6a$, $-2a$, and $5a$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6a - 2a + 5a + 3 &=  \\\\\n(6 - 2 + 5)a + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 9a + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231335 1069 "e-21975" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231335 1071 "$14m - 6 - 5m =$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231335 1072 #uuid "7f103ab6-84a8-5cb7-994a-14cec163530c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231335 1074 "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $14m$ and $-5m$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 14m - 6 - 5m \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{14m - 5m}_{m \\textrm{terms}} - 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{14m - 5m} - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= (14 - 5)m - 6 \\\\[5pt] &= 9m - 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231336 1069 "e-21976" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231336 1071 "$4m + 3m + 6n - 2n - 5 =$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231336 1072 #uuid "baeab473-d85f-5320-b4ac-1a981ecb49e4" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231336 1074 "We have two groups of two like terms, $4m$ and $3m$, and $6n$ and $-2n$.\n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine each group into a single term, adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\underbrace{4m + 3m}_{m \\textrm{terms}}\\,+\\, \\underbrace{6n - 2n}_{n \\textrm{terms}} - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= (4+3)m + (6 - 2)n - 5 \\\\[5pt] &= 7m + 4n - 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231337 1069 "e-21977" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231337 1071 "$(7 + 4y)\\cdot 6=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231337 1072 #uuid "1333e06d-19a6-5862-b8f5-6d82b8131a04" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231337 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(7 + 4y) \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 6 + 4y \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 42 + 24y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231338 1069 "e-21978" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231338 1071 "$4(3a + 7b)=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231338 1072 #uuid "7da54966-8a23-5849-8f23-4a4a43441719" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231338 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(3a + 7b) &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 3a + 4 \\cdot 7b &=  \\\\\n&= 12a + 28b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231339 1069 "e-21979" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231339 1071 "$(9x - 4)\\cdot 5=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231339 1072 #uuid "a05a2775-0dfc-500a-93b3-808df6aba208" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231339 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x - 4) \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n9x \\cdot 5 - 4 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 45x - 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231340 1069 "e-21980" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231340 1071 "$6(2p - 5q)=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231340 1072 #uuid "1ec9bb9c-6470-573e-bc3b-b2ffe110b9e4" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231340 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6(2p - 5q) &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2p - 6 \\cdot 5q &=  \\\\\n&= 12p - 30q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231341 1069 "e-21986" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231341 1071 "$4x^2y'' + 3x(x+2)y' - 2y = 0$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n)$, given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n$a_n = \\dfrac{3(1-2n)}{f(n)}\\,a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$4x^{2}y^{″}|= 4(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 3x(x + 2)y^{′}|= 6λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[3(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} + 6(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n},; - 2y|=-2a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2a_{n}x^{λ + n}$.*" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231341 1072 #uuid "e21115ff-5dbd-5e50-a0b4-d6d76cd23031" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231341 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as a power series with the\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}n=0\n$$\n\nterm isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2}y^{″} &= 4(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n3x(x + 2)y^{′} &= 6λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[3(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} + 6(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n- 2y &= -2a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + 6λa_{0}x^{λ} - 2a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(4λ^{2} - 4λ + 6λ - 2)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= 2(2λ^{2} + λ - 1)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n)) + 3(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} + 6(λ + n)a_{n} - 2a_{n}]x^{λ + n} &=  \\\\\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[4(λ + n)^{2} + 2(λ + n)a_{n} - 2a_{n} + 3(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}]x^{λ + n} &=  \\\\\n&= 2x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(2(λ + n)^{2} + (λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + \\frac{3}{2}(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}]x^{n} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2λ^{2} + λ - 1)a_{0}x^{λ} + 2x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(2(λ + n)^{2} + (λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + \\frac{3}{2}(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(2λ^{2} + λ - 1)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(2(λ + n)^{2} + (λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + \\frac{3}{2}(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2λ^{2} + λ - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(2λ - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= -1, \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$, we take\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq1\n$$\n\nequal to $0$, we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2(λ + n)^{2} + (λ + n) - 1)a_{n} + \\frac{3}{2}(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n((λ + n) + 1)(2(λ + n) - 1)a_{n} &= -\\frac{3}{2}(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand solving for $a_n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((\\frac{1}{2} + n) + 1)(2(\\frac{1}{2} + n) - 1)a_{n} &= -\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2} + n - 1)a_{n - 1} \\\\\n(n + \\frac{3}{2})(2n)a_{n} &= -\\frac{3}{2}(n - \\frac{1}{2})a_{n - 1} \\\\\nn(2n + 3)a_{n} &= \\frac{3}{4}(1 - 2n)a_{n - 1} \\\\\na_{n} &= (3(1 - 2n))/(4n(2n + 3))a_{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n) = 4n(2n+3)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231342 1069 "e-21987" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231342 1071 "$32x+12=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231342 1072 #uuid "dde22095-fb3c-589c-9174-9685cc7763d3" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231342 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(32x, 12) = 4$, so\n$32x + 12|=; (4 \\cdot 8x) + (4 \\cdot 3) \\mid$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot 8x) + (4 \\cdot 3)|=; 4(8x + 3)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(8x+3)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231343 1069 "e-21988" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231343 1071 "$63x-36=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231343 1072 #uuid "3d1a2ea4-b152-5344-9985-70db3c8b729d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231343 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(63x,36) = 9$, so:\n$63x - 36|=; (9 \\cdot 7x) - (9 \\cdot 4)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(9 \\cdot 7x) - (9 \\cdot 4)|=; 9(7x - 4)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $9(7x-4)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231344 1069 "e-21989" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231344 1071 "$4x+20y-12=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231344 1072 #uuid "9b3effd3-744a-5a49-983d-4c7b99b68bb1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231344 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4x,20y,12) = 4$, so\n$4x + 20y - 12|=; (4 \\cdot x) + (4 \\cdot 5y) - (4 \\cdot 3)$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot x) + (4 \\cdot 5y) - (4 \\cdot 3)|=; 4(x + 5y - 3)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(x + 5y - 3)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231345 1069 "e-21990" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231345 1071 "What is the area of the square shown below?\n\n![](images/53/53ab7a043ebbe03245fd0f8e1bf6438897a30d91654b6e51194205e4c302c07f.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231345 1072 #uuid "768e8723-fc4c-5d5e-afdb-47a48d301f02" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231345 1074 "The area of a square is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = (\\textrm{Side Length})^2\n$$\n\nNote that each side of a grid cell measures $3\\,\\textrm{ft}$, and each side of the shaded square covers $4$ grid cells. So, the length of each side of the shaded square is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Side Length} = 4 \\times 3\\:\\textrm{ft} = 12 \\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= (12ft)^{2} \\\\\n&= 12ft \\cdot 12ft \\\\\n&= (12 \\cdot 12)(ft \\cdot ft) \\\\\n&= 144ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231346 1069 "e-21991" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231346 1071 "A cinema has a rectangular hall whose dimensions are shown in the figure below. What is the area of the hall?\n\n![](images/e7/e7263eb3d75ecb243301c2d6c5712dd77232b97b6839123febec3260b389b2e6.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231346 1072 #uuid "e63912c0-70d4-5c77-bac4-6572911835ec" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231346 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\text{Length} \\times \\text{Width}\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Length} = 4.5\\:\\text{m}, \\qquad \\text{Width} = 6\\:\\text{m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= 4.5m \\cdot 6m \\\\\n&= (4.5 \\cdot 6)(m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 27m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231347 1069 "e-21992" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231347 1071 "The area of the rectangular field shown below is $2,400\\,\\textrm{m}^2$. If the length of the rectangle is $60\\:\\textrm{m}$, what is its width?\n\n![](images/16/167b4637cf2a7dd371c71a088471868c9109f2e5b4391184eb8a6ede13cd42aa.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231347 1072 #uuid "dfa9de39-6947-5333-b8d1-7333f7796383" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231347 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the length and $b$ is the width.\n\nFrom the figure, we see that\n\n$$\na = 60\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad \\mathcal{A} = 2,400\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula and solving for $b$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n2, 400 &= 60 \\cdot b \\\\\n\\frac{2, 400}{60} &= b \\\\\n40 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width is $40\\:\\textrm{m}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231348 1069 "e-21994" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231348 1071 "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $4(3x-7)$?\n\n1. $12x-28$\n2. $5x-7-2x$\n3. $6x+6x-32$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231348 1072 #uuid "0d388b06-7b37-5c1e-b8bd-82a56a1be461" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231348 1074 "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $4(3x-7)$, we first collect like terms and then factor each result:\n\n- We factor out the greatest common factor in expression I. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,28)=4$, we get\n$12x - 28|=; (4 \\cdot 3x) - (4 \\cdot 7)|=; 4 \\cdot (3x - 7) \\mid.✓$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression II, $5x$ and $-2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$5x - 7 - 2x|=; 5x - 2x - 7|=; (5 - 2)x - 7|=; 3x - 7$.\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(3x,7)=1$, there is nothing to factor out.\n$3x - 7 \\qquad{\\color{red}\\times}$\nSo, expression II *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression III, $6x$ and $6x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$6x + 6x - 32|=; (6 + 6)x - 32|=; 12x - 32$.\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,32)=4$, we get\n$12x - 32|=; (4 \\cdot 3x) - (4 \\cdot 8)|=; 4 \\cdot (3x - 8) \\mid.\\cdot$\nSo, expression III *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231349 1069 "e-21995" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231349 1071 "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $30x+25$?\n\n1. $5(9x+5-3x)$\n2. $3(8x+4+2x)$\n3. $10(4x-2x+5)$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231349 1072 #uuid "70af5973-bf6a-57f7-b2e3-b4d6036e3123" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231349 1074 "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $30x+25$, we first collect like terms and then distribute the number outside each pair of parentheses:\n\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression I, $9x$ and $-3x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$9x + 5 - 3x|=; 9x - 3x + 5|=; (9 - 3)x + 5|=; 6x + 5$.\nSo, expression I is equivalent to\n$5(9x + 5 - 3x)|=; 5(6x + 5)$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5{:}$\n$5(6x + 5)|=; (5 \\cdot 6x) + (5 \\cdot 5)|=; 30x + 25 \\mid ✓$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression II, $8x$ and $2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$8x + 4 + 2x|=; 8x + 2x + 4|=; (8 + 2)x + 4|=; 10x + 4$.\nSo, expression II is equivalent to\n$3(8x + 4 + 2x)|=; 3(10x + 4)$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $3{:}$\n$3(10x + 4)|=; (3 \\cdot 10x) + (3 \\cdot 4)|=; 30x + 12 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo, expression II *is not* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression III, $4x$ and $-2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$4x - 2x + 5|=; (4 - 2)x + 5|=; 2x + 5$.\nSo, expression III is equivalent to\n$10(4x - 2x + 5)|=; 10(2x + 5)$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $10{:}$\n$10(2x + 5)|=; (10 \\cdot 2x) + (10 \\cdot 5)|=; 20x + 50 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo, expression III *is not* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231350 1069 "e-21996" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231350 1071 "$8b+5+3(4b-6)=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231350 1072 #uuid "692b61e4-432e-5984-a7dc-4d601d770e42" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231350 1074 "First, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8b + 5 + 3(4b - 6) &=  \\\\\n8b + 5 + 3 \\cdot 4b - 3 \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 8b + 5 + 12b - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting expression has two groups like terms, $8b$ and $12b$, and $5$ and $-18$. Combining the terms in each group, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8b + 5 + 12b - 18 &=  \\\\\n8b + 12b + 5 - 18 &=  \\\\\n(8 + 12)b + (5 - 18) &=  \\\\\n&= 20b - 13 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(20b,13)=1\n$$\n\nthere is nothing to factor out.\n\n$$\n20b - 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression is equivalent to $20b-13$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231351 1069 "e-22003" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231351 1071 "![](images/f1/f104bbe9aafd070b0baa2137e8aa89e8c1cc0c12210ab31b4bb5a5c299ad5bf5.png)\n\nMaria is designing a square patio whose dimensions are shown in the figure above. What is the area of the patio?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231351 1072 #uuid "39ef66a3-0763-5b08-9ca9-0ca2f8f67dfd" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231351 1074 "The area of a square is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = (\\textrm{Side Length})^2\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Side Length} = 7\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= (7m)^{2} \\\\\n&= 7m \\cdot 7m \\\\\n&= (7 \\cdot 7)(m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 49m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231352 1069 "e-22004" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231352 1071 "Which value is a solution to the equation $m + 6 = 14$?\n\n1. $m=6$\n2. $m=7$\n3. $m=8$\n4. $m=9$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231352 1072 #uuid "0ae7fa58-1728-5f3d-990f-514298ddf77d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231352 1074 "A solution to a linear equation is a value (or set of values) that makes the equation true when you substitute it in place of the variable.\n\nNotice that if we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\ninto the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm + 6 &= 14 \\\\\n8 + 6 &= 14 \\\\\n14 &= 14✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll other options result in a false statement. Therefore, the solution is $m = 8$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231353 1069 "e-22005" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231353 1071 "Which value is a solution to the equation $18 - k = 11$?\n\n1. $k=5$\n2. $k=6$\n3. $k=7$\n4. $k=8$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231353 1072 #uuid "800cd873-b219-52f6-badd-d1b851e44a69" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231353 1074 "A solution to a linear equation is a value (or set of values) that makes the equation true when you substitute it in place of the variable.\n\nNotice that if we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{k}}={\\color{blue}{7}}\n$$\n\ninto the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 - k &= 11 \\\\\n18 - 7 &= 11 \\\\\n11 &= 11✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll other options result in a false statement. Therefore, the solution is $k = 7$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231354 1069 "e-22006" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231354 1071 "Which value is a solution to the equation $5p = 35$?\n\n1. $p=5$\n2. $p=6$\n3. $p=7$\n4. $p=8$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231354 1072 #uuid "85bd7acd-6b39-5b1c-9126-7761390de740" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231354 1074 "A solution to a linear equation is a value (or set of values) that makes the equation true when you substitute it in place of the variable.\n\nNotice that if we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{p}}={\\color{blue}{7}}\n$$\n\ninto the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5p &= 35 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 7 &= 35 \\\\\n35 &= 35✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll other options result in a false statement. Therefore, the solution is $p = 7$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231355 1069 "e-22007" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231355 1071 "What is the solution of the equation $\\dfrac{s}{5} = 9$?\n\n1. $s=45$\n2. $s=50$\n3. $s=55$\n4. $s=60$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231355 1072 #uuid "9c5460cf-3a2c-577f-bf80-fa40b0636380" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231355 1074 "A solution to a linear equation is a value (or set of values) that makes the equation true when you substitute it in place of the variable.\n\nNotice that if we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{s}}={\\color{blue}{45}}\n$$\n\ninto the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{s}{5} &= 9 \\\\\n\\frac{45}{5} &= 9 \\\\\n9 &= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo other values result in a true statement. Therefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ns = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{45}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231356 1069 "e-22008" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231356 1071 "If $5 + x = 12$, then $x=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231356 1072 #uuid "c1fc3ab7-4219-5a35-9118-2335a04c51a5" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231356 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, to isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite of adding* $5$ to $x$. So, we *subtract* $5$ from $x$, remembering to perform the same operation on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 + x &= 12 \\\\\n5 + x - 5 &= 12 - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, simplifying each side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5 - 5 &= 12 - 5 \\\\\nx + (5 - 5) &= 12 - 5 \\\\\nx + 0 &= 7 \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231357 1069 "e-22009" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231357 1071 "If $\\dfrac{5}{6} + y = \\dfrac{11}{6}$, then $y=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231357 1072 #uuid "3c34e4fe-74b1-557d-a2ac-c02fd6ee1ecc" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231357 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, to isolate $y$, we need to perform the *opposite of adding*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nto $y$. So, we *subtract*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nfrom $y$, remembering to perform the same operation on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6} + y &= \\frac{11}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{6} + y - \\frac{5}{6} &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, simplifying each side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} \\\\\ny + (\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{5}{6}) &= \\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} \\\\\ny + 0 &= \\frac{6}{6} \\\\\ny &= 6 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231358 1069 "e-22010" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231358 1071 "Given that $4m = 28$, determine the value of $m$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231358 1072 #uuid "1e2e4374-cb1d-59e2-8cb2-725b666ad16f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231358 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, the variable $m$ is *multiplied* by $4$. To isolate $m$, we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $4$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4m &= 28 \\\\\n4m \\div 4 &= 28 \\div 4 \\\\\n(4 \\div 4)m &= 7 \\\\\n1m &= 7 \\\\\nm &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231359 1069 "e-22012" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231359 1071 "Given that $45 = 9x$, determine the value of $x$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231359 1072 #uuid "a835868d-5dfe-5c84-98ae-36c84b1a4252" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231359 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n9x = 45\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is *multiplied* by $9$. To isolate $x$, we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $9$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x &= 45 \\\\\n9x \\div 9 &= 45 \\div 9 \\\\\n(9 \\div 9)x &= 5 \\\\\n1x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231360 1069 "e-22013" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231360 1071 "If $9a=20$, then $a=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231360 1072 #uuid "3397fe31-8696-5c69-9e00-33173d651299" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231360 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, the variable $a$ is *multiplied* by $9$. To isolate $a$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $9$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9a &= 20 \\\\\n9a \\div 9 &= 20 \\div 9 \\\\\n(9 \\div 9)a &= \\frac{20}{9} \\\\\n1a &= \\frac{20}{9} \\\\\na &= 20 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231361 1069 "e-22014" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231361 1071 "If $\\dfrac{p}8=15$, then $p=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231361 1072 #uuid "91d06ffd-f90d-587e-80c4-6796dc499472" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231361 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, the variable $p$ is *divided* by $8$. To isolate $p$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by $8$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{8} &= 15 \\\\\n\\frac{p}{8} \\cdot 8 &= 15 \\cdot 8 \\\\\np &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231362 1069 "e-22015" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231362 1071 "If $\\dfrac{4}{9}a=6$, then $a=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231362 1072 #uuid "96ff9c5d-9138-527a-9767-55caebca4379" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231362 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nThe opposite operation to multiplying by a fraction is multiplying by its reciprocal.\n\nIn this case, the variable $a$ is *multiplied* by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $a$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{9}a &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{4}{9}a \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} &= 6 \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\\n(\\frac{4}{9} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4})a &= \\frac{6}{4} \\cdot \\frac{9}{1} \\\\\n1a &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{1} \\\\\na &= 27 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231363 1069 "e-22018" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231363 1071 "If $16 = r + 9$, then $r=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231363 1072 #uuid "e7c81e10-0ca2-559e-80c4-1fb207779359" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231363 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\nr + 9 = 16\n$$\n\nIn this case, to isolate $r$, we need to perform the *opposite of adding* $9$ to $r$. So, we *subtract* $9$ from $r$, remembering to perform the same operation on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + 9 &= 16 \\\\\nr + 9 - 9 &= 16 - 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, simplifying each side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + (9 - 9) &= 16 - 9 \\\\\nr + 0 &= 7 \\\\\nr &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231364 1069 "e-22021" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231364 1071 "If $15=8b$, then $b=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231364 1072 #uuid "47431676-c1d4-5b21-bece-9df284a915e6" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231364 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n$8b=15$\n\nIn this case, the variable $b$ is *multiplied* by $8$. To isolate $b$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $8$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8b &= 15 \\\\\n8b \\div 8 &= 15 \\div 8 \\\\\n(8 \\div 8)b &= \\frac{15}{8} \\\\\n1b &= \\frac{15}{8} \\\\\nb &= 15 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231365 1069 "e-22022" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231365 1071 "If $5=\\dfrac{x}{9}$, then $x=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231365 1072 #uuid "f2eb3474-6e89-5b66-b226-4639595cd777" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231365 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}9 = 5\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is *divided* by $9$. To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by $9$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{9} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{9} \\cdot 9 &= 5 \\cdot 9 \\\\\nx &= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231366 1069 "e-22023" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231366 1071 "If $\\dfrac{k}{10}=\\dfrac25$, then $k=$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231366 1072 #uuid "11e30b27-af7e-54db-800e-93f27235220f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231366 1074 "To solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it, performing each inverse operation on both sides of the equation step by step.\n\nIn this case, the variable $k$ is *divided* by $10$. To isolate $k$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by $10$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{10} &= \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{k}{10} \\cdot 10 &= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot 10 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{20}{5} \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231367 1069 "e-22026" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231367 1071 "A person's bank account has a balance of $-80$ dollars. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The number $-80$ is negative.\n2. The person has an $80$ dollar debt to the bank.\n3. The bank owes $80$ dollars to the person." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231367 1072 #uuid "e88f9570-f73f-5b68-918b-e04ebf51715e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231367 1074 "For an account with a balance of $-80$ dollars, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid -80 \\mid = 80\n$$\n\nwhich represents the size of the **debt** in dollars.\n\nThus, we have the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The number $-80$ is negative.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The person has an $80$ dollar **debt** to the bank.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231368 1069 "e-22032" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231368 1071 "Solve for $t$ given that $t - 3.7 = 5.6$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231368 1072 #uuid "af0b86eb-9b9a-5639-b825-87c1319f3f64" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231368 1074 "To isolate $t$, we need to perform the opposite of subtracting $3.7$ from $t$, which is adding $3.7$ to $t$.\n\nSo, we add $3.7$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt - 3.7 &= 5.6 \\\\\nt - 3.7 + 3.7 &= 5.6 + 3.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt + 0 &= 9.3 \\\\\nt &= 9.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231369 1069 "e-22033" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231369 1071 "A water tank contained $w$ liters of water at the beginning of the day. After $9.6$ liters were drained, $14.7$ liters remained. What is the value of $w$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231369 1072 #uuid "b4c4ec5c-e17e-56a3-9f91-253f6545aed1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231369 1074 "The tank started with $w$ liters of water. Then, $9.6$ liters were drained, so the amount of water remaining can be written as\n\n$$\nw - 9.6\n$$\n\nSince $14.7$ liters remained, we equate this expression to $14.7$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nw - 9.6 = 14.7\n$$\n\nTo isolate $w$, we need to perform the opposite of subtracting $9.6$ from $w$, which is adding $9.6$ to $w$.\n\nSo, we add $9.6$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw - 9.6 &= 14.7 \\\\\nw - 9.6 + 9.6 &= 14.7 + 9.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw + 0 &= 24.3 \\\\\nw &= 24.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tank originally contained $24.3$ liters of water." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231370 1069 "e-22034" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231370 1071 "A bakery makes $x$ trays of muffins each morning. Each tray contains $6$ muffins. The bakery must bake at least $72$ muffins in the morning. Which inequality represents this situation?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231370 1072 #uuid "dc5ddbbd-fdec-57d3-b91b-64c55d7cf315" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231370 1074 "We are told that the bakery makes $x$ trays each morning.\n\nSince each tray contains $6$ muffins, the total number of muffins baked can be written as\n\n$$\n6x\n$$\n\nBecause the bakery must bake *at least* $72$ muffins, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Total muffins baked & \\ge 72 \\\\ 6x & \\ge 72\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n6x \\geq 72\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231371 1069 "e-22036" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231371 1071 "Solve for $z$ given that $z - \\dfrac{5}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{6}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231371 1072 #uuid "d942fa3d-9673-5201-8979-93aad98f44e7" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231371 1074 "To isolate $z$, we need to perform the opposite of subtracting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nfrom $z$, which is adding\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nto $z$.\n\nSo, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$\n\nto both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - \\frac{5}{12} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\nz - \\frac{5}{12} + \\frac{5}{12} &= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 0 &= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{12} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2}{12} + \\frac{5}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 5}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231372 1069 "e-22041" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231372 1071 "Solve for $x$ given that $4.8 - x = 9.1$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231372 1072 #uuid "53f0049e-05d6-54d6-9eca-6d95f8278f08" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231372 1074 "To isolate $x$, we subtract $4.8$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.8 - x &= 9.1 \\\\\n4.8 - x - 4.8 &= 9.1 - 4.8 \\\\\n4.8 - 4.8 - x &= 9.1 - 4.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 - x &= 4.3 \\\\\n- x &= 4.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $-x$ (the opposite of $x$) is equal to $4.3$. Thus, $x$ must be equal to the opposite of $4.3$, which is $-4.3$.\n\nTherefore, $x = -4.3$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231373 1069 "e-22042" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231373 1071 "Solve for $x$ given that $\\dfrac{9}{10} - x = \\dfrac{17}{10}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231373 1072 #uuid "7654a8ad-c2b1-52c2-9325-3187986d07a9" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231373 1074 "To isolate $x$, we subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nfrom both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{9}{10} - x &= \\frac{17}{10} \\\\\n\\frac{9}{10} - x - \\frac{9}{10} &= \\frac{17}{10} - \\frac{9}{10} \\\\\n\\frac{9}{10} - \\frac{9}{10} - x &= \\frac{17}{10} - \\frac{9}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 - x &= \\frac{17}{10} - \\frac{9}{10} \\\\\n- x &= \\frac{17 - 9}{10} \\\\\n- x &= \\frac{8}{10} \\\\\n- x &= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $-x$ (the opposite of $x$) is equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nThus, $x$ must be equal to the opposite of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231374 1069 "e-22044" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231374 1071 "A planter box is made from a rectangular concrete block that is $\\dfrac{9}{2} \\, \\textrm{ft}$ long, $\\dfrac{5}{3} \\, \\textrm{ft}$ wide, and $2 \\, \\textrm{ft}$ high. If each cubic foot of concrete requires $8\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sand, how much sand was used to make the planter box?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231374 1072 #uuid "335c26f1-f4da-5614-8f33-8ab7d53e01e8" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231374 1074 "First, we make a sketch of the planter box.\n\n![](images/a1/a14a7234f921eed85149d45800430f9d0bb01e4ee796c36d5ba2711cee4a412f.png)\n\nThe volume of the planter box is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{90}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the above fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{90 \\div 6}{6 \\div 6} = 15 \\, \\textrm{ft}^3\n$$\n\nSince each cubic foot of concrete requires $8\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sand, we get\n\n$$\n8 \\times 15 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sand were used to make the planter box." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231375 1069 "e-22070" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231375 1071 "A water tank contained $w$ liters of water at the start of the day. After $7.4$ liters were drained, $13.6$ liters remained. Which equation can be used to find $w$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231375 1072 #uuid "d7401b2b-94ef-5f1e-8149-80ec766b7678" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231375 1074 "The tank started with $w$ liters of water. Then, $7.4$ liters were drained, so the amount of water remaining can be written as\n\n$$\nw - 7.4\n$$\n\nSince $13.6$ liters remained, we equate this expression to $13.6$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\nw - 7.4 = 13.6\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231376 1069 "e-22072" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231376 1071 "A total of $p$ pencils are packed equally into $6$ boxes. If each box contains $9$ pencils, which equation can be used to find $p$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231376 1072 #uuid "fddd81b5-eb95-50fb-a16d-e98ca9e24db1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231376 1074 "If $p$ pencils are packed equally into $6$ boxes, then the number of pencils in each box can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{6}\n$$\n\nSince each box contains $9$ pencils, we equate this expression to $9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{6} = 9\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231377 1069 "e-22073" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231377 1071 "A recipe uses $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ of a cup of oil for each batch of cookies. If a baker used a total of $4$ cups of oil, which equation can be used to find the number of batches, $b$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231377 1072 #uuid "1ead43f9-4d27-51d9-883f-12b0471d7d3c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231377 1074 "Each batch requires\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nof a cup of oil. If the baker makes $b$ batches, then the total amount of oil used can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}b\n$$\n\nSince the baker used a total of $4$ cups of oil, we equate this expression to $4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}b = 4\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231378 1069 "e-22084" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231378 1071 "Each notebook costs $4$ dollars. A student buys $x$ notebooks, and the total cost is less than $30$ dollars. Which inequality represents this situation?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231378 1072 #uuid "388740cd-4146-5009-a691-c564a80b21c4" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231378 1074 "If each notebook costs $4$ dollars and a student buys $x$ notebooks, then the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\n4x\n$$\n\nBecause the total cost is *less than* $30$ dollars, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Total cost & < 30 \\\\ 4x & < 30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n4x < 30\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231379 1069 "e-22085" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231379 1071 "Each batch of paint uses $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ liter of blue dye. If an artist mixes $x$ batches, the total amount of blue dye used is less than $6$ liters. Which inequality represents this situation?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231379 1072 #uuid "bc85aac2-ff83-57d0-ac2f-7fbb976ede47" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231379 1074 "Each batch requires\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nliter of blue dye. If the artist mixes $x$ batches, then the total amount of blue dye used can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x\n$$\n\nBecause the total amount of blue dye used is *less than* $6$ liters, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Total dye used & < 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x & < 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x < 6\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231380 1069 "e-22086" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231380 1071 "A bakery places $6$ muffins into each box. If the bakery packs a total of $48$ muffins, how many boxes did the bakery fill?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231380 1072 #uuid "fd333f61-073a-5876-91aa-d1c5b8e86629" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231380 1074 "Each box holds $6$ muffins. If the bakery fills $b$ boxes, then the total number of muffins packed can be written as\n\n$$\n6b\n$$\n\nSince the bakery packed a total of $48$ muffins, we equate this expression to $48$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\n6b = 48\n$$\n\nTo isolate $b$, we divide both sides of the equation by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6b &= 48 \\\\\n\\frac{6b}{6} &= \\frac{48}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the bakery filled $8$ boxes." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231381 1069 "e-22087" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231381 1071 "A ribbon of length $L$ meters is cut into $6$ equal pieces. If each piece is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ of a meter long, what is the length of the ribbon?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231381 1072 #uuid "2d399fe7-181b-5d8f-ae93-6123b483da01" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231381 1074 "If a ribbon of length $L$ meters is cut into $6$ equal pieces, then the length of each piece can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{L}{6}\n$$\n\nSince each piece is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nof a meter long, we equate this expression to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{L}{6} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $L$, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{L}{6} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{L}{6} \\cdot 6 &= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the ribbon is $4$ meters." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231382 1069 "e-22126" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231382 1071 "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,4\\}$ and suppose\n$f(-1)=3,\\qquad f(0)=-2,\\qquad f(2)=5,\\qquad f(4)=1$.\nCompute the following:\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231382 1072 #uuid "6308dcce-94b4-57ff-9d03-a184ad2048bb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231382 1074 "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that smallest output occurs.\n\nThe outputs of $f$ on $S$ are\n$3,\\qquad -2,\\qquad 5,\\qquad 1$.\nThe largest output is $5$, and it occurs at $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5, \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=2\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the smallest output is $-2$, and it occurs at $x=0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-2, \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=0\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231383 1069 "e-22127" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231383 1071 "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n$f(-1)=4,\\qquad f(0)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(3)=2$.\nCompute $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231383 1072 #uuid "b8e4dcaf-38c1-5244-9bca-2e0eab32c34c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231383 1074 "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $4,1,4$, and $2$. The maximum output value is $4$.\n\nThis maximum occurs at both $x=-1$ and $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\{-1,2\\}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231384 1069 "e-22128" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231384 1071 "Let $S$ be a set and let $f$ be a real-valued function on $S$. Consider the expression\n$x^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$.\nWhat does this statement mean in words? Also, what does $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ represent?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231384 1072 #uuid "c901e488-7c50-5af5-85b2-ce70b1447a8c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231384 1074 "The expression\n\n$$\nx^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)\n$$\n\nmeans that:\n\n- $x^*$ is an input value in $S$.\n- Among all $x \\in S$, the value $f(x^*)$ is as large as possible.\n\nIn other words, $x^*$ is a choice of input where $f$ attains its largest value on $S$.\n\nBy contrast, $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is not an input. It is the largest *function value* that $f$ attains on $S$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231385 1069 "e-22129" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231385 1071 "| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $3$ |\n| $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of $f$ on the set $S=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$. Use the table to compute\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231385 1072 #uuid "de77d46f-0d04-510d-951e-650c9ae456fa" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231385 1074 "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values in the table are $3,0,5,-1$, and $2$.\n\nThe largest output is $5$, and it occurs at $x=0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=0\n$$\n\nThe smallest output is $-1$, and it occurs at $x=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-1 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=1\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231386 1069 "e-22130" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231386 1071 "![](images/eb/eb6079ac70816cd56061b8b353cc734176eed1698c50376a2e7a34c40f582a89.png)\n\nLet $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}$. The graph above shows the value of $f(x)$ for each $x \\in S$. Use the graph to compute\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231386 1072 #uuid "5fe9ded3-34fb-5bf2-a2f4-9598e6cf2d57" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231386 1074 "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, the highest point has $y$-coordinate $5$, and it occurs at $x=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{5} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}\n$$\n\nThe lowest point has $y$-coordinate $0$, and it occurs at $x=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231387 1069 "e-22720" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231387 1071 "What fraction is represented on the number line depicted below?\n\n![](images/83/83c666cb77f3efdcb7bf8b679b0ee9bc6b251f5ab6434414efac5a920e113b51.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231387 1072 #uuid "63a70c0e-e086-5c2f-99b1-748224da9d29" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231387 1074 "Notice that the line is split into $\\color{blue}8$ equal parts.\n\nThe number we are interested in is located $\\color{red}3$ steps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/02/02d63047e0bf53e4bce5284f930e30094a505ab7dd940bcabc2373d7f289af05.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231388 1069 "e-22723" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231388 1071 "What mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/01/01fef7b2b78f818dd3283724bb0a4017dc58737cdd9e9776102a1843aa7e7e9b.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231388 1072 #uuid "03b9f3d4-8a2d-544f-906a-7eadc57fe0f8" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231388 1074 "The line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/16/16e325fdd532d15842c64b86dbab526493fc02edaa2cd42afbbd106531aea8b6.png)\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making $\\color{red}2$ whole steps plus $\\color{blue}3$ part steps (out of a possible ${\\color{blue}{4}}$).\n\n![](images/bb/bb4f223aa301323171507c89658f4a2377804bccfc216be2de497eb10fd8edff.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{4}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231389 1069 "e-23330" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231389 1071 "How many feet are in $216$ inches?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231389 1072 #uuid "c537983b-01c2-5bd3-92cd-52a642114128" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231389 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between feet and inches:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{ft} = 12 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from inches to feet, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\,\\textrm{ft} = 1 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $216$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $216{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n216 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft &= 216 \\cdot 1in \\\\\n\\frac{216}{12}ft &= 216in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{216}{12}\n$$\n\nas the division $216 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  |  | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $12$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  |  | $\\phantom{0}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $216\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $18\\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231390 1069 "e-23331" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231390 1071 "How many yards are in $408$ inches?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231390 1072 #uuid "4d70b5d5-d67d-546c-bd3f-04c6dca4822d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231390 1074 "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$$408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$$34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231391 1069 "e-23338" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231391 1071 "What is the surface area of the triangular prism represented by the net below?\n\n![](images/da/dafb07440f60418465b579aad73119cd47d6def34ed0cf1229afc9affbb177e4.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231391 1072 #uuid "d795bee0-09be-59f9-bf46-91a6cad9070f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231391 1074 "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (side faces).\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 2 \\cdot (Area of base) + 3 \\cdot (Area of side) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 12 + 3 \\cdot 48 \\\\\n&= 24 + 144 \\\\\n&= 168cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231392 1069 "e-23339" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231392 1071 "Maggy is building a pyramid from a cardboard net, shown below. What will be the total surface area of the pyramid?\n\n![](images/29/29c918514e60da13363883770befd9d80098deaba0b45cfcafa3255feb143261.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231392 1072 #uuid "1cd2d853-090c-531f-9630-35b89d60faa0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231392 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ triangular base and $3$ triangular sides.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of base) + 3 \\cdot (Area of side) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 10 + 3 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 10 + 36 \\\\\n&= 46in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231393 1069 "e-23340" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231393 1071 "![](images/fe/fe43d65b35d583b21f25977a738e758a31d6b04eb08957877879cc54eef3f45f.png)\n\nAssuming that all of the side faces have the same height $(8\\,\\text{in})$, find the total surface area of the square pyramid above by considering its net." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231393 1072 #uuid "a55728da-7e24-5203-a230-4a75abd112f2" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231393 1074 "The net of our pyramid is as follows:\n\n![](images/c4/c4a5fe995aaaf47b7ab2b90492059d739fc2352955dc1e12a71852fc13046a33.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with sides equal to $5 \\, \\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 5 \\times 5 = 25 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nEach side face of our solid is a triangle, where the base is $5\\,\\text{in}$ and the height is $8\\,\\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times 8 = 20 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (the base) and $4$ triangles (the side faces).\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of the square) + 4 \\cdot (Area of the triangle) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 25 + 4 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 25 + 80 \\\\\n&= 105in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231394 1069 "e-2369" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231394 1071 "A tour guide has created a list of various ancient artifacts located inside and outside a cave in a park. The distance is measured from the entrance of the cave, with positive values representing distances outside the cave and negative values representing distances inside the cave. The data is shown below.\n\n| Artifact Point | Distance (m) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| A | $-10$ |\n| C | $20$ |\n| B | $-50$ |\n| D | $60$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Point A is $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ inside the cave.\n2. Point A is further inside the cave than point D.\n3. Point A is further inside the cave than any other point." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231394 1072 #uuid "91f6db00-317a-5e09-8d57-09cbba7be653" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231394 1074 "In this context,\n\n- positive numbers represent distances outside the cave, and\n- negative numbers represent distances inside the cave.\n\nThe absolute value gives the size of the distance from the cave entrance.\n\nLet's examine each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since point A has a location of $-10$, and negative values represent positions inside the cave, point A is $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ inside the cave.\n- Statement II is true. Point D has a location of $60$, which means it is outside the cave. Since point A is inside the cave and point D is outside, point A is further inside the cave than point D.\n- Statement III is false. Point A is $\\mid -10 \\mid = 10\\,\\textrm{m}$ inside the cave, while point B is $\\mid -50 \\mid = 50\\,\\textrm{m}$ inside the cave. Since $50 > 10$, point B is further inside the cave than point A.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231395 1069 "e-2370" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231395 1071 "A sea diver finds a huge sea animal $50 \\, \\textrm{m}$ deep in the sea. The diver records the location of the animal as $-50 \\, \\textrm{m}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. In this context, $0$ represents the location of the sea level.\n2. The absolute value of the location of the animal gives the depth at which the animal is found.\n3. If the location of the second sea animal is $-30 \\, \\textrm{m}$, then it is further to the sea level than the first one." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231395 1072 #uuid "32604952-0f62-5ac7-b74a-3df13c8568cb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231395 1074 "The depth of the first sea animal can be represented using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/76/768a3e83fe5321be4c9903747124accfdf1806b87cdaf31df91ccb7fd0619240.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the diver records a location of $-50\\,\\textrm{m}$ to represent $50\\,\\textrm{m}$ below sea level, the value $0$ must represent sea level.\n- Statement II is true. Taking the absolute value, $\\mid -50\\,\\textrm{m} \\mid = 50\\,\\textrm{m}$. The absolute value gives the depth of the animal below sea level.\n- Statement III is false. If the second sea animal is located at $-30\\,\\textrm{m}$, then $\\mid -30 \\mid = 30$ and $\\mid -50 \\mid = 50$. Since $30 < 50$, the second sea animal is closer to sea level than the first one.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231396 1069 "e-2382" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231396 1071 "Order the following set of numbers from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$-4, \\; 5, \\; -1, \\; 6, \\; 2$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231396 1072 #uuid "a89bc116-2ee6-50b4-a158-6ea58d46d341" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231396 1074 "First, we plot all of these numbers on a number line:\n\n![](images/5a/5a8f4feb9101618af6d402e7897d27686734472d24ca445935daa388a5046a7f.png)\n\nGoing from left to right, we have:\n$-4< -1 < 2 < 5 < 6$\n\nFinally, we can write the numbers in order, from least to greatest:\n$-4, \\; -1, \\; 2, \\; 5, \\; 6$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231397 1069 "e-2383" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231397 1071 "Order the following set of numbers from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$-\\dfrac{7}{6},\\;3,\\;-2, \\; \\dfrac{3}{2}$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231397 1072 #uuid "d2d15dd0-566e-5698-9221-a909835d95a9" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231397 1074 "The lowest common denominator of all fractions in the given list is $6$.\n\nTo begin with, we write all of the numbers as fractions with a denominator of $6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{6}, \\; \\dfrac{18}{6}, \\; -\\dfrac{12}{6}, \\; \\dfrac{9}{6}\n$$\n\nFor the negative fractions, we can bring the negative sign inside the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-7)}{6}, \\; \\dfrac{18}{6},\\; \\dfrac{(-12)}{6}, \\; \\dfrac{9}{6}\n$$\n\nNow, to order the given list from *least* to *greatest*, we order the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-12)}{6} < \\dfrac{(-7)}{6} < \\dfrac{9}{6} < \\dfrac{18}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we change each fraction back to its original form:\n\n$$\n-2 < -\\dfrac{7}{6} < \\dfrac{3}{2} < 3\n$$\n\nFinally, the required ordered list is:\n\n$$\n-2, \\; -\\dfrac{7}{6}, \\; \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\; 3\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231398 1069 "e-2384" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231398 1071 "Order the following set of numbers from *greatest* to *least*:\n\n$4,\\; -\\dfrac 6 5,\\; 3,\\;-0.8,\\; -1$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231398 1072 #uuid "a913d38c-5c1a-534a-8eb2-15f388b35fe6" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231398 1074 "First, we write the decimal $(-0.8)$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n4,\\; -\\dfrac 6 5,\\; 3,\\;-\\dfrac 8 {10},\\; -1\n$$\n\nNext, we write all of the numbers as fractions with a denominator of $10$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{10},\\;-\\dfrac{12}{10}, \\; \\dfrac{30}{10},\\;-\\dfrac{8}{10}, \\; -\\dfrac{10}{10}\n$$\n\nFor the negative fractions, we can bring the negative sign inside the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{10}, \\; \\dfrac{(-12)}{10},\\;\\dfrac{30}{10}, \\; \\dfrac{(-8)}{10}, \\; \\dfrac{(-10)}{10}\n$$\n\nNow, to order the given list from *greatest* to *least*, we order the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{10} > \\dfrac{30}{10} > \\dfrac{(-8)}{10} > \\dfrac{(-10)}{10} > \\dfrac{(-12)}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we change each fraction back to its original form:\n\n$$\n4 > 3 > -0.8 > -1 > -\\dfrac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required ordered list is:\n\n$$\n4,\\; 3,\\; -0.8,\\; -1,\\; -\\dfrac 6 5\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231399 1069 "e-24987" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231399 1071 "![](images/85/859346f030ff97fb72bf790e7cb2dc3d549498e9741736544c11ffc1d5339068.png)\n\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral shown above (not drawn to scale)?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231399 1072 #uuid "7995ac56-83a8-5f46-826b-cead0dcbee09" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231399 1074 "The perimeter of a polygon is the total distance around its boundary, so we add the lengths of all its sides.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the quadrilateral is\n\n$$\nP = 8 + 13 + 14 + 15 = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{50} \\: \\text{m}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231400 1069 "e-24988" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231400 1071 "What is the perimeter of a rectangle with a length of $14\\:\\text{ft}$ and a height of $9\\:\\text{ft}$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231400 1072 #uuid "a79556df-d21a-5434-bb10-ebbf9f2b59a1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231400 1074 "The perimeter of a polygon is the total distance around its boundary, so we add the lengths of all its sides.\n\nRecall that a rectangle is a quadrilateral with two pairs of equal parallel sides and four right angles.\n\n![](images/dd/dd3d5d78be792f3e20b89eb2b9996867f942e7dfb52574a819bfa95b0c33a3fd.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 2 \\cdot (Length + Height) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (14 + 9) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 23 \\\\\n&= 46ft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231401 1069 "e-24989" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231401 1071 "What is the perimeter of a rhombus with side length $9\\:\\text{cm}$?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231401 1072 #uuid "8ee5770f-42b5-5912-ac86-bdf799c31ab0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231401 1074 "The perimeter of a polygon is the total distance around its boundary, so we add the lengths of all its sides.\n\nRecall that a rhombus is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, where all four sides are equal.\n\n![](images/37/375ebbce04120f1deb36481de8d8d5dcff962f0ec75a4e187e5bc189bafe3d08.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rhombus is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 4 \\cdot (Side Length) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 36cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231402 1069 "e-25086" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231402 1071 "Liam wants to cover a tent roof that has the form of a square pyramid, as shown above. Find the total surface area that he needs to cover if all four sides have the same surface area. Note that Liam does *not* need to cover the base.\n\n![](images/37/375623158fda9ddb30c51ea42751d4509090df1dc6f62e492a881a6636ede2d9.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231402 1072 #uuid "a66ec0ea-45be-5158-81dc-67cff568bf42" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231402 1074 "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n![](images/6d/6d49696589fa941f586dbc4df5e20925fabe6ed18e57ce45078baa9a17087edf.png)\n\nEach side face of our solid is a triangle, where the base is $7\\,\\text{m}$ and the height is $6\\,\\text{m}$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 7 \\times 6 = 21 \\, \\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nNow, the total area of the side surfaces consists of $4$ triangular faces.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area of the sides &= 4 \\cdot (Area of the triangle) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 84m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, Liam needs to cover $84 \\, \\text{m}^2$ of the tent roof." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231403 1069 "e-2634" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231403 1071 "Find the area of the triangle.\n\n![](images/df/df1f4c3b57d61ae82d2b1ae0242c2b567913b52967d1663092445f4a9d46bd53.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231403 1072 #uuid "5821763d-6de9-557f-9688-a690b09082ce" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231403 1074 "First, we find the height and base of the triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbase &= 6 \\\\\nheight &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 28 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in inches, the area is measured in square inches. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = 14 \\, \\textrm{in}^2\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231404 1069 "e-2636" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231404 1071 "A triangular garden plot has a base of $6$ meters and a height of $8$ meters. What is the area of the garden plot?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231404 1072 #uuid "6a25b580-e4dc-51c4-b807-87810e4a5deb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231404 1074 "Let's sketch our garden plot based on the description.\n\n![](images/1d/1d2972b8c13d0101a8ea94944fa0139a755f99ef04e889c2289a30abbffac2c6.png)\n\nThe height and base of the triangle are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbase &= 6, \\\\\nheight &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in meters, the area is measured in square meters. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = 24 \\, \\textrm{m}^2\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231405 1069 "e-2637" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231405 1071 "Find the length of the base of a triangle given that its height is $8\\, \\mathrm{m}$ and its area is $30\\, \\mathrm{m^2}$.\n\n![](images/1e/1ec176c21985eed95936475844044a763e24ba0949f69852279f2c801cb2c360.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231405 1072 #uuid "72173b80-8f7e-5e3f-8185-974455c90509" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231405 1074 "Let's write down what we know:\n\n$$\nHeight = 8; Area = 30\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the area and height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot 8 \\\\\n30 &= 4 \\cdot base\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = 30 \\div 4 = \\dfrac{30}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since the area is measured in square meters and the height is measured in meters, the base is measured in meters. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = \\dfrac{15}{2}\\, \\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231406 1069 "e-2775" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231406 1071 "Alessandro wants to build a concrete tank to store water. If he decides that the inner dimensions of this tank should be $2 \\, \\textrm{m}$ long, $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\, \\textrm{m}$ wide, and $\\dfrac{3}{2} \\, \\textrm{m}$ high, then how much water can this tank store?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231406 1072 #uuid "76094270-a732-5d90-a1be-0170702277a0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231406 1074 "First, we make a drawing of the tank.\n\n![](images/df/df81c8a71f853b0837a51481d6fe6a08ee1915e23d4b82ebbcf5cd12ec2802bb.png)\n\nThe volume of the tank is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{6} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tank will be able to store $4 \\, \\textrm{m}^3$ of water." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231407 1071 "![](images/8c/8cd6315b3514881342696a846469c897e8a171cc95f201cc25d0c7e716e97363.png)\n\nWhat fraction is represented on the number line above?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231407 1072 #uuid "016e02d3-b7b9-50c0-8ae7-ceb15f4c019f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231407 1074 "Notice that the line is split into $\\color{blue}12$ equal parts.\n\nThe number we are interested in is located $\\color{red}1$ step from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/1d/1d2d211cbe1b77c8550c53d5db28c9af15fdc7ef7cc80ad793bcb3e7c6f78030.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231408 1071 "What mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/22/22d1b303ecbd7efe2f623e78e8ac95c8c6d32e5fbedef3085787002dd850112f.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231408 1072 #uuid "b2f687d7-4824-529a-bb8f-d8389635fd0e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231408 1074 "The line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/9a/9abf61dfad134585ffbd0f094a3b854904c8a46c007065df80710baa1eb27c86.png)\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making $\\color{red}2$ whole steps plus $\\color{blue}1$ part step:\n\n![](images/b5/b5d8f4f0a4c001f08fad1369cc77550d15d7421bab7c2a928cd964eebf2389a3.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231409 1069 "e-6664" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231409 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t)$, if\n$y''+ y = u_1(t), \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0$,\n\nwhere $u_1(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231409 1072 #uuid "7ff7cc10-3649-5ffe-9a8a-6aedd7954ea5" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231409 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition property of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″} + y} &= L{u_{1}(t)} \\\\\nL{y^{″}} + L{y} &= L{u_{1}(t)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y \\right\\}=Y(s)\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y''\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'' \\right\\} = s^2 \\mathcal{L}\\{y \\} - s y(0) - y'(0) = s^2 Y(s) - s y(0) - y'(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\} = s^2 Y(s)\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{e^{-s}}{s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″}} + L{y} &= L{u_{1}(t)} \\\\\ns^{2}Y(s) + Y(s) &= \\frac{e^{-s}}{s} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 1)Y(s) &= \\frac{e^{-s}}{s} \\\\\nY(s) &= (e^{-s})/(s(s^{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1069 "e-6735" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1072 #uuid "bb405211-5b18-5cc1-a264-bdf6e2fbcc60" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1074 "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5$. Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p$. Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050736 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231578 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231581 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1039 "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1041 #edb/ref 17592186231302 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231411 1042 #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049330 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231579 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231580 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1039 "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1041 #edb/ref 17592186231298 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1042 #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231413 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048402 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231413 1038 #edb/ref 17592186231583 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231413 1038 #edb/