 17592186045585 true] [17592186231988 1062 #uuid "0f90e140-62d4-5e5a-b2cc-04b385179001" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231988 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231525 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231989 1064 "Previously, we considered the differential equation\n\n$$\n4xy'' + 2y' - y = 0\n$$\n\nand, using the Frobenius trial solution\n\n$$\ny=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\nwe found that the possible values of $\\lambda$ are $\\lambda=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$, we take\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nLet's now determine a **recurrence relation** for the coefficients of a Frobenius series solution of our previous example, for the case $\\lambda \\in (0,1)$.\n\nRewriting each term of the ODE as a power series (with the\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}n=0\n$$\n\nterm isolated), we obtained\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4xy^{″} &= 4(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1}, \\\\\n2y^{′} &= 2λa_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1}, \\\\\n- y &= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nSince we already handled the\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nterms in the previous slide to obtain the indicial equation, we focus only on the coefficients of the\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nterms here.\n\nFor\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\ncollecting like powers of $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n} + 2(λ + n)a_{n} - a_{n - 1}]x^{λ + n - 1} &=  \\\\\nx^{λ - 1}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[4((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n} + 2(λ + n)a_{n} - a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{λ - 1}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(4(λ + n)^{2} - 2(λ + n))a_{n} - a_{n - 1}]x^{n} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nFor each\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nthe coefficient of $x^n$ must be $0$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4(λ + n)^{2} - 2(λ + n))a_{n} - a_{n - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + n)(4(λ + n) - 2)a_{n} - a_{n - 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are working in the case $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2} + n)(4(\\frac{1}{2} + n) - 2)a_{n} &= a_{n - 1} \\\\\n4n(n + \\frac{1}{2})a_{n} &= a_{n - 1} \\\\\n2n(2n + 1)a_{n} &= a_{n - 1} \\\\\na_{n} &= (a_{n - 1})/(2n(2n + 1)), n \\ge 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231989 1065 14710 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231989 1066 #uuid "c8268534-e946-58bc-bba7-05c05e081ac1" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231989 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231990 1064 "We know how to find a Fourier-series particular solution $y_p$ for a periodically forced linear ODE.\nIn this topic, the new step is how to use $y_p$ to satisfy an initial condition.\n\nRecall that once a particular solution $y_p$ is chosen, the full solution has the form\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nNow, suppose the IVP is given at $x=x_0{:}$\n$y(x_0)=y_0$.\nThen,\n\n$$\ny_0 = y_c(x_0)+y_p(x_0)\n$$\n\nA useful simplification is to choose the particular solution so that\n$y_p(x_0)=0$.\nIn that case, the initial condition becomes\n\n$$\ny_0 = y_c(x_0)\n$$\n\nso we can solve for the constant(s) in $y_c$ immediately (without evaluating any Fourier sums at $x_0$).\n\nOn the next slide, we’ll apply this idea in an example." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231990 1065 14713 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231990 1066 #uuid "4a6bfce3-0ee4-5ea7-905f-461e3f15500e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231990 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231991 1064 "A standard vibrating system under an external force $f(t)$ looks like\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural **internal frequency**.\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ describes the system's internal oscillation when there is no external force. It can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\omega t) + B\\sin(\\omega t)\n$$\n\nso the motion naturally oscillates at frequency $\\omega$.\n\nIf the external force $f(t)$ is periodic, then it has its own forcing frequency $\\omega_e$. **Resonance** occurs when the forcing frequency matches the natural frequency:\n\n$$\n\\omega_e = \\omega\n$$\n\nIntuitively, resonance happens because the force repeatedly \"pushes\" the system at exactly the right rhythm each cycle. In the undamped model, these pushes add energy coherently over time, so the amplitude of the oscillations increases without bound.\n\nFor example, suppose the position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = -6\\cos^2{pt}\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Let's find the value of the parameter $p$ that causes the oscillator to resonate.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution has frequency $\\omega$, where\n\n$$\n\\omega^2=36\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\omega=6$.\n\nThe external force is\n\n$$\nf(t)=-6\\cos^2{pt} = -3(1+2\\cos{2pt})\n$$\n\nwhich has forcing frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega_e=2p\n$$\n\nResonance occurs when\n\n$$\n\\omega_e=\\omega\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{\\omega}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231991 1065 14762 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231991 1066 #uuid "b61c68de-4314-5d91-bcb2-75fd1ed98232" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231991 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231992 1064 "In this topic, we will use Fourier series to solve boundary value problems with periodic forcing functions.\n\nAs before, we consider a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe main ideas are similar to those of solving IVPs:\n\n- we choose the correct form of the series for the particular solution $y_p$, and then\n- use boundary conditions at the endpoints to determine the constants in the homogeneous part $y_c$ of the solution.\n\nWhen $f(x)$ is periodic, we often look for $y_p$ as a Fourier series on a symmetric interval $[-L,L]$.\n\nRecall that for an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no (damping) $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the parity of $f(x)$:\n\n- If $f$ is *even*, then its Fourier series contains only cosine terms.\n- If $f$ is *odd*, then its Fourier series contains only sine terms.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. We want the appropriate Fourier series form for a particular solution $y_p$.\n\nBecause $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so the Fourier basis on this interval is\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. Since the equation has no $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the same parity and is also odd.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231992 1065 14775 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231992 1066 #uuid "67a0ca11-7450-5f6a-8bdc-4aee6301f209" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231992 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231993 1064 "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=0\n$$\n\nand $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n1. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c$, so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D$, we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= C\\cos (ωt) + D\\sin (ωt) + t(-Cω\\sin (ωt) + Dω\\cos (ωt)) \\\\\ny_{p}^{″} &= 2(-Cω\\sin (ωt) + Dω\\cos (ωt)) - tω^{2}(C\\cos (ωt) + D\\sin (ωt))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\ny_{p}^{″} + ω^{2}y_{p} = 2ωD\\cos (ωt) - 2ωC\\sin (ωt)\n$$\n\nSince we need\n\n$$\ny_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)\n$$\n\nequating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a, \\qquad -2\\omega C=b\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega}, \\qquad D=\\frac{a}{2\\omega}\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSo, when\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)\n$$\n\nand resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231993 1065 14777 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231993 1066 #uuid "b19a33b7-fd81-538c-8139-d44653c47533" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231993 1067 "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations With Resonance" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231994 1064 "Recall that resonance is the phenomenon where an external periodic force drives a vibrating system at exactly the system's natural frequency. In the undamped model, this causes the oscillations to grow larger and larger over time.\n\nIn the ideal undamped model, resonance produces a particular solution that grows in magnitude with time (due to the extra factor of $t$). This is why the model predicts that the amplitude increases *without bound*.\n\nFor example, consider the undamped forced oscillator\n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = \\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(3t) + B\\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nthe natural frequency of the system is $\\omega = 3$, and the forcing frequency is also $3$. Because these frequencies match, resonance occurs.\n\nSince the forcing term $\\sin(3t)$ duplicates a term in the homogeneous solution, we must multiply our guess by $t$. We try a solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = t(C\\cos(3t) + D\\sin(3t))\n$$\n\nCalculating the derivatives of $y_p$, substituting them into the equation, and equating coefficients of $\\sin(3t)$ and $\\cos(3t)$ yields\n\n$$\nC=-\\dfrac16\n$$\n\nand $D=0$. So, the particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = -\\dfrac{t}{6}\\cos(3t)\n$$\n\nNotice that this solution grows larger as $t$ increases. This time-dependent growth illustrates how resonance leads to an increase in oscillation amplitude in the undamped model.\n\nIn real systems, perfectly *unbounded growth* does not occur because physical effects, such as damping and energy loss, eventually limit the amplitude. Even so, forcing near the natural frequency can still produce very large oscillations, which is why resonance is important in engineering design." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231994 1065 14778 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231994 1066 #uuid "4f439c3c-eac7-5d41-a8b1-fcbe2eb8d44b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231994 1067 "Practical Resonance" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231995 1064 "When we solve a second-order IVP using Fourier series, we still represent the solution in the form\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nbut since the homogeneous solution has *two* constants, we use *two* conditions:\n\n$$\ny(0)=y_0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(0)=y_1\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified. Here, we have two cases:\n\n- **Case 1:** $f(x)$ is odd.If $f(x)$ is *odd* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *odd*, so it is a sine series:\n$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$\nThis immediately gives $y_p(0)=0$, so the condition $y(0)=y_0$ determines the cosine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n- **Case 2:** $f(x)$ is even.If $f(x)$ is *even* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *even*, so it is a cosine series:\n$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$\nHere, the derivative is a sine series:\n$y_p'(x)=-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{L} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$\nThis immediately gives $y_p'(0)=0$, so the condition $y'(0)=y_1$ determines the sine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n\nIn both cases, the key idea is that evaluating at $x=0$ (and differentiating once) lets the initial conditions pick off the constants in $y_c$ as cleanly as possible.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice on the next slide." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231995 1065 14781 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231995 1066 #uuid "c8c0ab68-168a-57f1-9ab6-13bd9d042d32" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231995 1067 "Second-Order IVPs" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231996 1064 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''+ 7y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3,\\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is an even, $2\\pi$-periodic function. Since the forcing is even and the differential operator contains no $y'$ term, we represent the particular solution as a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+7y=0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2+7=0\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x)\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{7}\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sqrt{7}\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition $y'(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(0) &= -A\\sqrt{7}\\sin (0) + B\\sqrt{7}\\cos (0) - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}\\sin (0) \\\\\n0 &= 0 + B\\sqrt{7} - 0 \\\\\nB &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0$.\n\nNext, we apply $y(0)=3$. With $B=0$, the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= A + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n} \\\\\nA &= 3 - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)\\left(3-\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\right) +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231996 1065 14782 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231996 1066 #uuid "edbe311b-6f4f-5c28-96fa-394b23f30390" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231996 1067 "A Worked Example of a Second-Order IVP" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231997 1064 "In this lesson, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to derive a second-order differential equation that governs the current $I(t)$ in an RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn a series RCL circuit, Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE(t)=V_R+V_C+V_L\n$$\n\nFor the three components:\n\n- Resistor: the voltage drop is proportional to the current,\n$V_R=RI$.\n- Capacitor: the charge $q$ and capacitor voltage satisfy $q=CV_C$, so\n$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}$.\n- Inductor: the voltage drop is proportional to the rate of change of current,\n$V_L=L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nSubstituting these into Kirchhoff's voltage law gives\n\n$$\nE=RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTo obtain a differential equation in terms of $I$ only, we first differentiate both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(E) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(RI + \\frac{q}{C} + L\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n\\frac{dE}{dt} &= R\\frac{dI}{dt} + \\frac{1}{C} \\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt} + L\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the current $I(t)$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q(t)$, measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I\n$$\n\nwe get the second-order differential equation governing the current:\n\n$$\nL\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{C}I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ gives an equivalent form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac{R}{L}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{LC}I=\\dfrac{1}{L} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nLet's see a more concrete derivation in the next example." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231997 1065 14791 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231997 1066 #uuid "e9875681-c31f-535a-b3e2-7fcec32a16e3" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231997 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231998 1064 "Recall that we are considering a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThere are many types of boundary conditions (including mixed or Robin-type conditions), but in this lesson, we will focus on the two simplest cases:\n\n- *Dirichlet conditions* (values of $y$ are specified):\n$y(a)=\\alpha \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y(b)=\\beta$\n- *Neumann conditions* (values of $y'$ are specified):\n$y'(a)=\\alpha \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(b)=\\beta$\n\nA key simplification is possible if we choose our particular solution $y_p$ to satisfy the corresponding *homogeneous* boundary conditions. For any given $f(x)$, it is always possible to find such a $y_p$.\nThis drastically simplifies the computations, as the boundary conditions then determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\n- *Dirichlet-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n$y_p(a)=0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y_p(b)=0$,\nthen\n$y(a)=y_c(a) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y(b)=y_c(b)$,\nso we can solve for the constants in $y_c$ using only the homogeneous solution.\n- *Neumann-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n$y_p'(a)=0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y_p'(b)=0$,\nthen\n$y'(a)=y_c'(a) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(b)=y_c'(b)$,\nso the derivative conditions determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231998 1065 14795 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231998 1066 #uuid "16e90dd1-2074-5728-ad7b-e05a00c3e171" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231998 1067 "Using Boundary Conditions" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231999 1064 "For example, consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'-2y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Suppose we choose the forcing (and hence the Fourier-series particular solution) so that\n$y_p(0)=0$.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y_c = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nA $2$-periodic forcing function can be written using harmonics of frequency $\\pi$, so we may write the particular solution in the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right)\n$$\n\nThus the full solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=Ae^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right)\n$$\n\nNow apply $y(0)=3$ and use $y_p(0)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= Ae^{2(0)} + y_{p}(0) \\\\\n3 &= A(1) + 0 \\\\\nA &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ny(x)= 3e^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right)\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231999 1065 14804 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231999 1066 #uuid "ae6c43bc-e554-5975-9433-c7d67c2adbdd" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186231999 1067 "A Worked Example" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232000 1064 "When we solve a second-order differential equation for the current $I(t)$, we often write the solution in the form\n\n$$\nI(t)=I_c(t)+I_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $I_c(t)$ is the **transient current**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty$, and\n- $I_p(t)$ is the **steady-state current**, which governs the current’s long-term behavior.\n\nTo determine a unique solution for $I(t)$, we must specify two initial conditions, typically $I(0)$ and $I'(0)$.\n\nThe current is often given directly as an initial condition, for example, $I(0)=I_0$. We are also often given the initial *capacitor charge*, $q(0)=q_0$.\n\nTo find the other condition $I'(0)$, we use Kirchhoff's voltage law equation:\n\n$$\nE(t) = RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSolving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{1}{L}E - \\dfrac{R}{L}I - \\dfrac{q}{LC}\n$$\n\nNow evaluate at $t=0{:}$\n\n$$\nI'(0) = \\dfrac{1}{L}E(0) - \\dfrac{R}{L}I(0) - \\dfrac{1}{LC}q(0)\n$$\n\nSo, if the initial charge $q(0)$ and initial current $I(0)$ are given, we can compute $I'(0)$ by substituting those values into the formula above.\n\nIn the next slide, we'll determine the initial conditions of a differential equation that governs the current in a given circuit." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232000 1065 14808 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232000 1066 #uuid "a6f06e46-ed1e-5e49-a9f8-679033a08a0f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232000 1067 "Initial Conditions for Solving the ODE Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232001 1064 "In previous lessons, we solved second-order initial value problems using Laplace transforms when the forcing term was an ordinary function of $t$.\n\nIn this lesson, we extend the same method to **time-delayed forcing**, where the right-hand side turns on at a specific time. This type of forcing is modeled using the **unit step function**.\n\nFor a fixed constant $a>0$, we write\n\n$$\nu_a(t) = u(t-a)\n$$\n\nwhich represents a signal that is zero for $t< a$ and turns on at $t = a$. For example, $u(t-3)$ remains zero until $t=3$, at which point it jumps to $1$.\n\nWhen a second-order differential equation includes a term involving $u_a(t)$, we still begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and solving for\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}\n$$\n\n(for $s>0$). The left-hand side is handled exactly as before, using the Laplace transforms of derivatives and the given initial conditions.\n\nThe main new idea is how the unit step function affects the transform of the forcing term. We will see how to account for this delay and how it changes the algebraic equation for $Y(s)$. To do this, we first need to determine the transform of the step function itself. Let's find some Laplace transforms." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232001 1065 14810 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232001 1066 #uuid "932cf723-31f5-5d76-95e4-d384feb59c11" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232001 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232002 1064 "A **line of symmetry** for a figure is a line such that folding the figure along the line produces two matching halves.\n\nFor example, let's consider the polygon below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b06aec40ae4294314de28c6b51b60cf8f5b3fb2c101e01862f7f5e1f8b53fd0.png)\n\nThis polygon has a line of symmetry $l$, shown below.\n\n![](images/7b/7b9dca9bca387aa4389d545df3197f553db4fca0a047452178d7fd2e0accca70.png)\n\nNotice how folding the figure along $l$ produces two perfectly matching halves." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232002 1065 14818 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232002 1066 #uuid "298bf3cc-b3e4-5651-b502-bd5101e40b91" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232002 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232003 1064 "Some polygons don't have any lines of symmetry. For example, let's find how many lines of symmetry the polygon shown below has.\n\n![](images/c8/c8af8cd18ffcffdea7bb7b6ab4b57d6c123f045d4f3f0ce54c2f63db5b509efa.png)\n\nRecall that a *line of symmetry* for a figure is a line such that folding the figure along the line produces two matching halves.\n\nNo matter which lines we try, it is not possible to fold this polygon into matching parts. Therefore, the given polygon has no lines of symmetry.\n\nTo demonstrate, we can't consider all possible lines, but three attempts are shown below.\n\n- Folding along a horizontal line does not map the polygon onto itself.\n\n![](images/81/813ae64be2e5991dcd9c7eebaf19a2ed777367172f5c8c34166162188b76cc06.png)\n\n- Folding along a sloped line does not map the polygon onto itself either.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9f663b92603d6625b14d283239bfacc434adf8aafbfc6331f473644f304428.png)\n\n- Folding along a vertical line does not map the trapezoid onto itself either.\n\n![](images/82/8236ba5898240de236915602b4196f0b43061cfee9dd0fe0bd1e491f177ebbfa.png)\n\nWe could try other lines, but none of them will work." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232003 1065 14819 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232003 1066 #uuid "968c25eb-7f8c-5ea7-9035-b00233f4d9ef" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232003 1067 "Finding Lines of Symmetry" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232004 1064 "In the first-order case, we saw that a time delay in the forcing term appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as}$, and that we can handle this using the second shifting theorem.\n\nThe same idea applies when working with **second-order** differential equations.\n\nConsider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 4y = 7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nu_2(t)=u(t-2)\n$$\n\nis the unit step function.\n\nAgain, we begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and writing\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the initial conditions, the left-hand side becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}+4\\mathcal L\\{y\\}=s^2Y(s)+4Y(s)\n$$\n\nThe forcing term has the form\n$u_2(t)\\,f(t-2)$,\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(t-2)=7e^{-2(t-2)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad f(t)=7e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nAs in the first-order case, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. That is, if\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>0$.\n\nHere, using $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal L\\{7e^{-2t}\\}=\\frac{7}{s+2}\n$$\n\nso the transformed forcing term is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}\\} = e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{7}{s+2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the transformed differential equation gives\n\n$$\ns^2Y(s)+4Y(s)=\\frac{7e^{-2s}}{s+2}\n$$\n\nWe now solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s^{2} + 4)Y(s) &= \\frac{7e^{-2s}}{s + 2} \\\\\nY(s) &= (7e^{-2s})/((s + 2)(s^{2} + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nJust as in the first-order case, the delay introduces the factor $e^{-as}$. Once this factor is identified, the remaining steps follow the same algebraic process used for second-order equations without time delay." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232004 1065 14829 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232004 1066 #uuid "b7d1588f-46ce-5a23-a449-ceb6b8f25099" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232004 1067 "Time-Delayed Forcing and the Second Shifting Theorem" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232005 1064 "Once the Laplace transform has been found, solving a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing follows the same overall strategy as before: we take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nThe key difference is handling terms that include a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nConsider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nu_2(t)=u(t-2)\n$$\n\nis the unit step function.\n\nWe previously found the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nY(s)=e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}\n$$\n\nTo take the inverse Laplace transform, we first focus on the *base function*\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}\n$$\n\nUsing partial fraction decomposition, we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nNow we compute the inverse transform of $F(s)$ and call it $f(t){:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\!\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}\\right\\} = 1-\\cos t\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. Since\n\n$$\nY(s)=e^{-2s}F(s)\n$$\n\n, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-2s}F(s)\\} = u(t-2)\\cdot f(t-2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our result for $f(t)$, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-2)\\left(1-\\cos(t-2)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe important idea is that once the exponential factor $e^{-as}$ has been identified, we first find the inverse transform of the base function, then shift the result and multiply by the unit step function." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232005 1065 14830 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232005 1066 #uuid "10f6f136-0604-57e5-b635-1c8a5cfcf8da" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232005 1067 "Solving for the Laplace Transform With Step Function Forcing" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232006 1064 "Consider a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing, such as\n\n$$\ny'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nu_2(t)=u(t-2)\n$$\n\nis the unit step function.\n\n**Step 1: Transform the left-hand side.**\n\nWe begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides. Let\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the second derivative and the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0) = s^2Y(s)\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Transform the right-hand side.**\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, the unit step function satisfies\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u_2(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>0$.\n\n**Step 3: Solve for $Y(s)$.**\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation gives\n\n$$\ns^2Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}\n$$\n\nWe now collect terms and solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s^{2} + 1)Y(s) &= \\frac{e^{-2s}}{s} \\\\\nY(s) &= (e^{-2s})/(s(s^{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAside from the exponential factor $e^{-2s}$, the steps are exactly the same as in problems without time delay." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232006 1065 14834 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232006 1066 #uuid "f7be86fb-5367-564f-906a-5ce064205685" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232006 1067 "A Worked Example" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232007 1064 "Many word problems ask how many equal-sized pieces, groups, or containers can be made from a given amount. In these situations, we compare the *total amount available* to the *size of one group*, which can lead to dividing one fraction by another fraction.\n\nThe key idea is that division helps us answer the question: *How many of this size fit into that amount?*\n\nFor example, consider the following question: How many\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\n-liter bottles can be filled with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56\n$$\n\nliters of juice?\n\nThis question asks how many groups of size\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\nfit into a total amount of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56\n$$\n\n. To answer it, we divide the total amount by the amount in one group:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56 \\div \\dfrac25\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the **reciprocal** of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\n. So we rewrite the division as multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56 \\div \\dfrac25 = \\dfrac56 \\times \\dfrac52\n$$\n\nNow we multiply fractions by multiplying the numerators and multiplying the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56 \\times \\dfrac52 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 5}{6 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{25}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{12} = 2\\,\\textrm{1} = 2\\,\\dfrac1{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nbottles can be filled with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac56\n$$\n\nliters of juice.\n\nThis same process applies to all fraction word problems in this lesson: identify what is being divided, rewrite the division using a reciprocal, and then multiply." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232007 1065 14838 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232007 1066 #uuid "483642df-14bf-5f9b-b461-cdac95599aba" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232007 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232008 1064 "If the area of a rectangle is known and one side length is given, the missing side length can be found by dividing the area by the known side length. This idea works whether the area, the side length, or both are fractions.\n\nFor example, let's determine the missing side length in the area model below.\n\n|  | $\\dfrac25$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  |  |\n| $00$ | $\\dfrac57$ |\n|  |  |\n\nTo find the missing side length, we divide the area by the length of the known side:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\div \\dfrac25 = \\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions by multiplying the numerators and multiplying the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57 \\times \\dfrac52 = \\dfrac{5 \\times 5}{7 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing side length is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{14}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232008 1065 14839 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232008 1066 #uuid "b31e4ad9-5f22-53c6-b3dc-8674b3e9e97f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232008 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232009 1064 "We already know how to locate points with *integer coordinates* in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to locate $(2,-1)$, we\n\n- start at the origin $(0,0)$,\n- move $2$ units *right*, and\n- then move $1$ unit *down*.\n\nThe same rules still apply for points with *rational coordinates* (coordinates that are fractions or decimals):\n\n- The first coordinate, $x$, controls horizontal movement.\n- The second coordinate, $y$, controls vertical movement.\n\nBut when the grid is divided into, say, *halves* between whole numbers, each part represents $0.5$ of a unit.\nFor example, consider the point $Q$ below.\n\n![](images/c2/c27a0af88f3c8bdd52f2bbc705a349cde612e1e4d7c95ce85cbfed14c6035742.png)\n\nTo determine the coordinates of $Q$,\n\n- we start at the origin,\n- move $2.5$ units *left* (the negative $x$ direction), and\n- then $1.5$ units *up* (the positive $y$ direction).\n\n![](images/52/5251344febc29f29c4f9f67947049a7c37d57c06d93abe84f280771ac78e6e42.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{(-2.5,1.5)}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232009 1065 14840 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232009 1066 #uuid "93e339d2-5b21-506d-a576-7c6934a7e09b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232009 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232010 1064 "Just like negative whole numbers, we can also have negative fractions.\n\n![](images/25/254ec178ef6c912efa024cebafeebc959ba77b55b179aa2789528f6b2c5be19d.png)\n\nWe can find the absolute value of negative fractions, like\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n, in exactly the same way as whole numbers.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the absolute value of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nwe find the distance between that number and zero on a number line.\n\n![](images/51/5125aee5af4158ef077a10d474b4e7eeedc78d871dc6b17c05ec708dd43b52d1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the absolute value of a rational number, we can simply drop the negative sign. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{aligned} \\left|-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right &= \\dfrac{3}{2}. \\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232010 1065 14841 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232010 1066 #uuid "79a86afc-20eb-593e-8c4a-605c909fbaa3" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232010 1067 "Absolute Values of Negative Fractions" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232011 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to identify *negative* rational numbers on a number line. But first, let’s recall how to identify a rational number on the *positive* side.\n\nTo identify a fraction on a number line:\n\n- First, look at the segment from $0$ to $1$ and count how many *equal parts* it is split into.\n- Then, count how many of those parts we move from $0$ to reach the point.\n\nFor example, let's identify which improper fraction is shown on the number line below.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1159ba8624715d78432223d43a91248d100721f266b5cb66090528270c764a.png)\n\nNotice that the line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/65/658740cee2d783170d462595565248a01b21f9324a48a7f77fdb0942eb47e5d4.png)\n\nThe number we are interested in is located\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4}+3=\\color{red}7\n$$\n\nsteps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/84/84340cfad54fc0109078fbee47ed0a362bd91d8952615499e60d72898cf8cce4.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232011 1065 14842 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232011 1066 #uuid "f6d0455d-bbff-548d-b82a-62eba9dc47a7" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232011 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232012 1064 "In the previous examples, the grid was divided into *halves*, and each step represented $0.5$ of a unit.\n\nSimilarly, when a unit is split into $10$ equal parts, each small step will represent $0.1$. We still count from $0$, but now the distances are smaller and require more precision.\n\nFor instance, what are the coordinates of the point $Z$?\n\n![](images/6f/6f7fd97b32475b136ec17db66254fa5ee20b57eadd5b727d3bf2257ae8394356.png)\n\nTo find the coordinates of $Z$, we start at the origin.\n\n- First, to find the *$x$-coordinate*, we move $0.2$ units to the *right* (the positive x-direction).\n- Then, to find the *$y$-coordinate*, we move $0.4$ units *down* (the negative y-direction).\n\n![](images/26/266c7faaad34705772fb7610714fddadad7c4df2267ac4c11edadd308f78e70b.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $Z$ are $(0.2,-0.4)$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232012 1065 14844 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232012 1066 #uuid "72576710-d4a2-5c51-94a7-5113dd6a9572" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232012 1067 "Finer Divisions" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232013 1064 "Identifying negative fractions on a number line is very similar to identifying positive fractions.\n\n- We still look for equal parts and count how many steps it takes to reach the point.\n- But negative numbers are to the *left* of $0$, so we count steps from $0$ *right to left*, and the final answer will be *negative*.\n\nFor example, let's find out which fraction is shown on the number line below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b000e955b0aae2947a707fdf56862ce9675dc6ea045425b4568f5a78eea3c1.png)\n\nNotice that the interval between $0$ and $-1$ is split into $\\color{blue}7$ equal parts.\n\nThe number we are interested in is located $\\color{red}3$ steps from $0$ (right to left).\n\n![](images/c9/c99e07ed2b97a52897e8c24d11a18569f9eb5f8f5e7f421476d1425360c3233a.png)\n\nTherefore, since our number lies to the left of zero, the picture represents\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nLet's now see examples with improper fractions and mixed numbers." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232013 1065 14849 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232013 1066 #uuid "7dd607f1-f507-5e1b-89dd-b797247fd0fb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232013 1067 "Negative Rational Numbers on a Number Line" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232014 1064 "A **linear expression** is an algebraic expression that contains a variable, but the variable is only multiplied by a number or divided by a number.\n\nSome examples of linear expressions are $3n+5$,\n\n$$\nx-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{5}+2\n$$\n\nTo **evaluate** an algebraic expression, we replace the variable with the value that it represents and then simplify.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the expression $3n+5$ and we know that $n=6$.\nThen $n$ represents the number $6$, so we replace $n$ with $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3n + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot n + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 6 + 5 &=  \\\\\n18 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 23 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $n=6$, the value of the expression $3n+5$ is $23$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232014 1065 14850 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232014 1066 #uuid "26c98f1c-d032-527d-87b5-1d15c95b861d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232014 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232015 1064 "To evaluate an algebraic expression, we replace the variable with a given value and then simplify the resulting numerical expression.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the expression $2x^2$ when $x=3$. This means we replace $x$ with $3$ everywhere it appears.\n\nAfter replacing the variable, we simplify using the order of operations, so exponents are evaluated before multiplication and addition. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 9 &=  \\\\\n&= 18 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen an expression contains an exponent, remember that the exponent tells us how many times the base is multiplied by itself. For instance, $3^2$ means $3\\times3$, and $3^3$ means $3\\times3\\times3$.\n\nIn this lesson, you will practice evaluating expressions by carefully replacing variables with given values and simplifying step by step." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232015 1065 14851 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232015 1066 #uuid "587f93fc-7040-52f6-ac08-ad6fb52ca83a" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232015 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232016 1064 "When simplifying a linear expression, our goal is to combine like terms so that the expression has fewer terms and is easier to work with.\n\nRecall that like terms are terms that have the same variable part. Because the variable parts represent the same quantity, the terms can be added or subtracted.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $8x - 3x$. The terms $8x$ and $-3x$ are like terms, so we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 3x &=  \\\\\n(8 - 3)_(⏟)_(coefficients)x &=  \\\\\n&= 5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen combining like terms:\n\n- add the coefficients if the terms are being added, and\n- subtract the coefficients if one term is being subtracted.\n\nIn this lesson, you will practice simplifying expressions by combining like terms step by step.\n\nSometimes, a term may appear without a written number in front of the variable. For example, $x$ means we are adding $1x$, and $-x$ means we are subtracting $1x$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232016 1065 14860 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232016 1066 #uuid "79f4710f-b4fb-52da-9771-ede25d3f2877" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232016 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232017 1064 "The **distributive law** describes how multiplication works when it is applied to a sum inside parentheses.\n\nWhen a number is multiplied by a sum, the multiplication is **distributed** to each term in the sum. In other words, the number outside the parentheses multiplies every term inside the parentheses.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $3(2x + 5)$. The number $3$ multiplies both terms inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2x + 5) &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2x + 3 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 6x + 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter applying the distributive law, the parentheses are removed, and the expression is written as a sum of the resulting terms.\n\nIn this lesson, you will learn how to apply the distributive law to different linear expressions." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232017 1065 14862 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232017 1066 #uuid "49b67f5c-181d-5253-8e62-cb61c7e7e48b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232017 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232018 1064 "The distributive law can also be used when the expression inside the parentheses is a *difference*.\n\nWhen a number is multiplied by an expression with a minus sign, the number outside the parentheses still multiplies every term inside the parentheses. The subtraction sign stays between the terms.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $5(4x - 3)$. The number $5$ multiplies both $4x$ and $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(4x - 3) &=  \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4x - 5 \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 20x - 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter distributing, the parentheses are removed, and the expression is written as the difference of the resulting terms." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232018 1065 14863 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232018 1066 #uuid "cf84ec04-f4eb-57e3-9547-b2d79d813041" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232018 1067 "Dealing With a Minus Inside the Parentheses" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232019 1064 "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand this, consider the triangle below, with base $b$ and height $h$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0fa27c8446b799668c5493fa2049eb10550134724dc5e54aab8628a33857f0e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around it, as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aa8ae2b0c13e0710a8316c920a4d62e0fe822095311d11f4ca3381f022585725.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nThe area of the enclosing rectangle is $b \\cdot h$. Since the triangle occupies exactly half of this area, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232019 1065 14875 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232019 1066 #uuid "8b463fce-69c3-5e1f-b5c1-15b44aaf6f50" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232019 1067 "Proof of the Formula For the Area of a Triangle" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232020 1064 "So far, we have considered the area of a *right triangle*.\nBut what is the area of a *general* triangle, one that is not necessarily a right triangle?\n\nIt turns out that the same idea still works.\n\n> A formula for the area of a triangle is\n> $\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h$,\n> where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nThis formula works for *any* triangle.\n\nLet's use this formula to find the area of the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7c8eaf780eaa0559e8ac4dc2557a423b805705bfd3a081635f5284502896168.png)\n\nIn our triangle,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = 7 \\: \\text{ft}, \\qquad \\textrm{height} = 3 \\: \\text{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 10.5ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we'll show how to prove the formula for the area of a triangle.\n\nLet’s see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232020 1065 14876 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232020 1066 #uuid "35bf275f-a5b4-5e22-8a11-a01a18b1bd80" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232020 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232021 1064 "When we **factor** a linear expression, we rewrite it as a product.\n\nA common situation is when two terms share a greatest common factor (GCF).\nTo factor, we do two steps:\n\n1. Write each term as a product using the GCF.\n2. Pull the GCF out in front of parentheses.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's factor the expression $32x + 12$.\n\n1. First, find the greatest common factor: $\\textrm{GCF}(32x,12)=4$. Since $32x = 4 \\cdot 8x$ and $12 = 4\\cdot 3$, we separate that factor from each term as follows:\n$32x + 12|=; (4 \\cdot 8x) + (4 \\cdot 3)$\n2. Now the factor $4$ is common to both terms, so we place it in front of the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot 8x) + (4 \\cdot 3)|=; 4_(⏟)_(GCF)(8x + 3)$\n\nAfter factoring, we can check our work by using the distributive law on $4(8x+3)$ to get back $32x+12$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232021 1065 14877 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232021 1066 #uuid "2be45773-d6cf-524a-87bf-000ccb33c195" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232021 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232022 1064 "The **area** of a shape is a measure of the two-dimensional space it covers. For example, we use area to figure out how much paint is needed for a wall or how much carpet is needed for a floor.\n\nTo measure area, we use a **square unit**. A square unit is a square whose sides are 1 unit long. For instance, let's look at a square with a side length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}$.\n\n![](images/bc/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}}$, which we read as \"one square inch.\"\n\nOther common square units include:\n\n- One square centimeter ($1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$)\n- One square meter ($1\\,\\textrm{m}^2$)\n- One square foot ($1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$)\n\nIn the next slides, we will see how to find the area of larger shapes by counting how many square units fit inside them." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232022 1065 14878 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232022 1066 #uuid "84c5ef96-8c57-5cc0-9631-95f2c1dcf0ca" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232022 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232023 1064 "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square shown below. Notice that this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](images/69/6983dc8f375dda4b1b62fd75799d3c830a7e50cdedb27b120599bfbbd17dcf1a.png)\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\n![](images/4f/4fa1f42d4058c9cd372b973a4f35d382e6c6fa4114657e4b6b8b7a8603dbf44e.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each. So, the total number of grid squares is\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 9\n$$\n\nSince each grid square has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, the total area of the large square is $9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232023 1065 14879 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232023 1066 #uuid "d495978b-5237-559b-989a-b717419c2717" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232023 1067 "The Area of a Square" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232024 1064 "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b$, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nLet’s demonstrate this by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS$, shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66dceb1c078ffc133626c31a37a509b1e2eb3e56d74d4015a42e5a71dabfeff2.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {PS} = 6\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n&= 10cm \\cdot 6cm \\\\\n&= (10 \\cdot 6)(cm \\cdot cm) \\\\\n&= 60cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b$. For example, if we instead set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nthen we still obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b = 60\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nas before." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232024 1065 14880 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232024 1066 #uuid "912ddd1d-6e7e-58b6-a982-080b13c537de" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232024 1067 "The Area of a Rectangle" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232025 1064 "Two expressions are **equivalent** if they always have the same value, even if they look different.\n\nA useful way to check whether two expressions are equivalent is to simplify each one. If they can be written in the same form, they are equivalent. This often involves using techniques like *distributing*, *collecting like terms*, or *factoring*.\n\nFor example, let's check if $2(3x+4)$ and $8x+8-2x$ are equivalent.\n\nThe first expression, $2(3x+4)$, is already simplified in a factored form. We will now simplify the second expression, $8x+8-2x$, to see if we can get it into the same form.\n\nFirst, we simplify the second expression by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 8 - 2x &=  \\\\\n8x - 2x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(8 - 2)x + 8 &=  \\\\\n&= 6x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(6x,8)=2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3x) + (2 \\cdot 4) &=  \\\\\n&= 2(3x + 4) \\mid.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified form, $2(3x+4)$, matches the first expression.\n\nSince the expressions can be written in the same form, they are equivalent." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232025 1065 14881 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232025 1066 #uuid "c14a5fb5-5b73-55bd-8168-4faaf9dd97c0" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232025 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232026 1064 "Suppose a circuit with a constant EMF of\n\n$$\nE=8\\,\\textrm{V}\n$$\n\nresistance of\n\n$$\nR=6\\,\\Omega\n$$\n\ncapacitance of\n\n$$\nC=0.25\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nand inductance of\n\n$$\nL=2\\,\\textrm{H}\n$$\n\nsatisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law equation\n\n$$\n8=6I+4q+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's derive the differential equation for $I$ by differentiating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n0=6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I\n$$\n\nthe differential equation for $I$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0\n$$\n\nNow suppose the initial charge and current are\n\n$$\nq(0)=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand $I(0)=1$, respectively. Solving our first relation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 4-3I-2q\n$$\n\nThen, the initial conditions for the current we use to solve the ODE are $I(0)=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI^{′}(0) &= 4 - 3I(0) - 2q(0) \\\\\n&= 4 - 3 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the resulting initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0, \\qquad I(0) = 1, \\quad I'(0) = 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nI(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232026 1065 14901 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232026 1066 #uuid "02b0c1dd-4819-5337-bc05-870ded85d834" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232026 1067 "A Worked Example" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232027 1064 "Sometimes, like terms are not written next to each other. In these cases, we first rearrange the expression so that like terms are grouped together, and then we combine them.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $12r - 5 + 3r$. The two variable terms, $12r$ and $3r$, are like terms, but they are separated by a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the expression so that the like terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12r - 5 + 3r &=  \\\\\n&= 12r + 3r_(⏟)_(rterms) - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the like terms are grouped, we combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12r + 3r_(⏟) - 5 &=  \\\\\n(12 + 3)r - 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 15r - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging terms makes it easier to see which terms can be combined before simplifying the expression." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232027 1065 14914 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232027 1066 #uuid "8b887afb-7d8f-543c-abcb-514c964cf44f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232027 1067 "Rearranging Terms to Group Like Terms" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232028 1064 "Some linear expressions contain terms with different variables. Even though these terms may look similar, they are **not** like terms and cannot be combined with each other.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $6x - 2x + 5y + y$. The $x$ terms and the $y$ terms represent different quantities, so they must be combined separately.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 2x + 5y + y &=  \\\\\n&= 6x - 2x_(⏟)_(xterms) + 5y + y_(⏟)_(yterms)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now combine each group of like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 2x_(⏟)_() + 5y + y_(⏟)_() &=  \\\\\n(6 - 2)x + (5 + 1)y &=  \\\\\n&= 4x + 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen simplifying expressions with more than one variable, always group and combine like terms one variable at a time." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232028 1065 14915 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232028 1066 #uuid "78aa92f9-f4b8-53be-a338-c9c2c6897d67" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232028 1067 "Simplifying Expressions with More Than One Variable" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232029 1064 "In real-world situations, algebraic expressions are often used to model quantities such as distance, cost, or time. Evaluating the expression means replacing the variable with a given value and interpreting the result in the context of the situation.\n\nSuppose the total cost (in dollars) of renting a bike for $t$ hours is given by the expression $12t + 5$. The value $5$ represents a fixed fee, and $12t$ represents the hourly cost.\n\nTo find the total cost of renting the bike for $3$ hours, we replace $t$ with $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12t + 5 &=  \\\\\n12 \\cdot 3 + 5 &=  \\\\\n36 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 41 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe numerical result tells us the value of the quantity, and the context tells us how to interpret it. In this case, the total cost of renting the bike for $3$ hours is $\\[math]41$.\n\nWhen evaluating expressions in real-world contexts, always remember to\n\n- replace the variable with the given value,\n- simplify using the order of operations, and\n- interpret the final value using the appropriate units." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232029 1065 14916 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232029 1066 #uuid "9a3176a5-4497-54fc-b816-ab06d3cb9d55" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232029 1067 "Evaluating Expressions in Real-World Contexts" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232030 1064 "How can we find the missing side of a rectangle if we know its area and one of its side lengths?\n\n![](images/02/02a6095810d0345cfb7d541df402cd0ae5357ab8fd6cde6f4ca530d93cb6b267.png)\n\nThe *area of a rectangle* is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{A}$ is the area, and $a$ and $b$ are the side lengths.\n\nFrom the figure, we are given:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 96\\,\\mathrm{m}^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 12\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute these values into the area formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n96 &= a \\cdot 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $a$, we divide both sides of the equation by $12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{96}{12} &= \\frac{a \\cdot 12}{12} \\\\\n8 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the missing side is $8\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232030 1065 14921 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232030 1066 #uuid "854c5e03-cd1a-5719-84dc-0a38c915c47e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232030 1067 "Finding a Side Given Another Side and Area" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232031 1064 "![](images/69/6983dc8f375dda4b1b62fd75799d3c830a7e50cdedb27b120599bfbbd17dcf1a.png)\n\nAnother way to find the area is to use the formula for the *area of a square*:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = (\\textrm{Side Length})^2\n$$\n\nFor our square, the side length is $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$. Let's use the formula to calculate the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= (Side Length)^{2} \\\\\n&= (3cm)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3cm \\cdot 3cm \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 3)(cm \\cdot cm) \\\\\n&= 9cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the result we found by counting the grid squares!" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232031 1065 14922 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232031 1066 #uuid "d7d3d18b-1c37-57c6-8e22-fa3924f47002" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232031 1067 "Formula for the Area of a Square" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232032 1064 "A **solution** to a linear equation is a value (or set of values) that makes the equation true when you substitute it in place of the variable.\n\nTo check whether a value is a solution:\n\n- Step 1. Substitute the value for the variable.\n- Step 2. Simplify both sides of the equation.\n- Step 3. If the result is a true statement, then the value is a solution.\n\nFor example, consider the equation $x-4=9$.\n\nIf we check $x=13$, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x}}={\\color{blue}{13}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4 &= 9 \\\\\n13 - 4 &= 9 \\\\\n9 &= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the equation becomes a true statement, $x=13$ is a solution.\n\nNow, let's check $x=10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4 &= 9 \\\\\n10 - 4 &= 9 \\\\\n6 &= 9\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the equation becomes a false statement, $x=10$ is not a solution." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232032 1065 14925 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232032 1066 #uuid "caa21980-66fa-5fa9-a54e-bcad6255e560" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232032 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232033 1064 "To **solve** a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it.\n\nIf a number is **added** to the variable, we use the **inverse operation**, which is subtraction.\n\nWhatever operation we perform on one side of the equation, we must perform the same operation on the other side to keep the equation balanced.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\nx + 8 = 15\n$$\n\nSince $8$ is added to $x$, we subtract $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 8 &= 15 \\\\\nx + 8 - 8 &= 15 - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow simplify each side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + (8 - 8) &= 15 - 8 \\\\\nx + 0 &= 7 \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x = 7$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232033 1065 14926 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232033 1066 #uuid "09d24e05-9607-5248-aae9-a6c9b7f808c3" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232033 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232034 1064 "Recall that to solve a linear equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operations applied to it.\n\nSo if a variable is multiplied by a number, we use the opposite operation, which is division.\n\nWhatever operation we perform on one side of the equation, we must perform the same operation on the other side to keep the equation balanced.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n6x = 42\n$$\n\nSince $x$ is multiplied by $6$, we divide both sides by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x &= 42 \\\\\n6x \\div 6 &= 42 \\div 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow simplify each side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6 \\div 6)x &= 42 \\div 6 \\\\\n1x &= 7 \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: If the variable appears on the right-hand side (e.g., $15=3y$), you may first swap the left and right sides of the equation to get $3y=15$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232034 1065 14928 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232034 1066 #uuid "32356097-47d4-5e0f-8d9b-6f30c60240f2" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232034 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232035 1064 "A **one-step multiplication equation** is an equation like\n$6x=15$,\nwhere the variable is multiplied by a number.\n\nTo solve a one-step multiplication equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operation that is applied to it.\n\nFor example, in the equation above, the variable $x$ is *multiplied* by $6$. The opposite of multiplying by $6$ is *dividing* by $6$.\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the equation by $6$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x &= 15 \\\\\n6x \\div 6 &= 15 \\div 6 \\\\\n(6 \\div 6)x &= \\frac{15}{6} \\\\\n1x &= \\frac{15 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis isolates the variable, so we have found the solution. Notice that the solution is a fraction. When solving multiplication equations, the answer may be a fraction, even if the initial equation only contains whole numbers.\n\n**Note**: Dividing by the coefficient indeed removes it from the left-hand side. This is because dividing a product by a number is equivalent to dividing its coefficient:\n\n$$\n6x \\div 6 = (6 \\div 6)x\n$$\n\nRecall that if the variable appears on the right-hand side (e.g., $15=6x$), you may first swap the left and right sides of the equation to get $6x=15$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232035 1065 14930 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232035 1066 #uuid "3c686756-4239-5b1e-a4a6-8a0c6d723512" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232035 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232036 1064 "Sometimes, the coefficient of the variable in a multiplication equation is a fraction, such as in the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}x=2\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to undo multiplication by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWe could do this by dividing both sides by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nas before.\n\nHowever, dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its **reciprocal**. So, to solve a multiplication equation with a rational coefficient, we multiply both sides by the reciprocal of the coefficient.\n\nIn our case, the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nand simplify.\n\nOn the left-hand side, we can re-group the numbers to multiply the fractions first. On the right-hand side, we can multiply fractions by first swapping denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{5}x &= 2 \\\\\n\\frac{4}{5}x \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\\n(\\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4})x &= \\frac{2}{4} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} \\\\\n1x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis isolates the variable, so we have found the solution." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232036 1065 14931 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232036 1066 #uuid "e3482536-8ae3-5762-8071-48be0db3e653" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232036 1067 "One-Step Multiplication Equations With Rational Coefficients" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232037 1064 "Sometimes the variable is on the right-hand side of the equation, such as\n\n$$\n14 = 5 + u\n$$\n\nWe can make the equation easier to solve by swapping the left and right-hand sides:\n\n$$\n5 + u = 14\n$$\n\nNow we solve using inverse operations, as before. Since $5$ is added to $u$, we subtract $5$ from both sides and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 + u &= 14 \\\\\n5 + u - 5 &= 14 - 5 \\\\\nu + (5 - 5) &= 14 - 5 \\\\\nu + 0 &= 9 \\\\\nu &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $u = 9$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232037 1065 14950 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232037 1066 #uuid "35d70e97-d68d-5b04-9c97-fd25c2287e8d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232037 1067 "Equations With the Variable on the Right-Hand Side" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232038 1064 "A **one-step division equation** is an equation like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=7\n$$\n\nwhere the variable is divided by a number.\n\nTo solve a one-step division equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operation that is applied to it.\n\nFor example, in the equation above, the variable $x$ is *divided* by $5$. The opposite of dividing by $5$ is *multiplying* by $5$.\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{5} &= 7 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{5} \\cdot 5 &= 7 \\cdot 5 \\\\\nx &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis isolates the variable, so we have found the solution.\n\n**Note**: We can see how multiplying by the denominator removes it from the left-hand side by swapping denominators and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{5} \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{5} &=  \\\\\nx \\cdot 1 &=  \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that if the variable appears on the right-hand side $\\Big($e.g.,\n\n$$\n7=\\dfrac{x}5\\Big)\n$$\n\nyou may first swap the left and right sides of the equation to get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}5=7\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232038 1065 14976 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232038 1066 #uuid "bbedbed2-eef7-53a4-9a9c-98c6cf799230" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232038 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232039 1064 "Let's consider another example of interpreting the absolute value in context.\n\nSuppose an outdoor swimming pool opens only if the average temperature over the day is above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$. The table below shows the daily average temperatures above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$ during a week.\n\n| Day | Degrees above min. opening temp |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Monday | $-2$ |\n| Tuesday | $4$ |\n| Wednesday | $1$ |\n| Thursday | $-3$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Monday's average temperature was $2$ degrees more than the minimum required for the pool to open.\nII. Thursday had the lowest average temperature.\nIII. Tuesday's average temperature was greater than Wednesday's average temperature.\n\nIn this context, a negative number represents a deficit, and the absolute value of the deficit gives the number of degrees the temperature is below the minimum for the pool to open.\n\nLet's go through each statement:\n\n- Monday has a negative value, and so the average temperature has a deficit for the pool to open. The number of degrees the temperature is below the minimum for the pool to open is $\\mid -2 \\mid = 2$. Hence, statement I is false.\n- The day with the lowest average temperature is the day whose temperature is most to the left on a number line.Clearly, Thursday's average temperature is most to the left, and so it had the lowest temperature. Hence, statement II is true.\n- Tuesday's average temperature is $4$, while Wednesday's is $1$. Since $4 > 1$, Tuesday's average temperature was greater than Wednesday's average temperature. Hence, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232039 1065 14977 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232039 1066 #uuid "b3738000-8f79-5098-adee-3d6eae926dcc" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232039 1067 "Interpreting the Absolute Values in Context" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232040 1064 "Recall that to *solve* an equation, we isolate the variable by *reversing* the operations applied to it.\n\nIf a number is *subtracted* from the variable, we use the inverse operation, which is *addition*.\n\nWhatever operation we perform on one side of the equation, we must perform the same operation on the other side to keep the equation balanced.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to solve for $x$ given that\n\n$$\nx - 11 = 14\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the opposite of subtracting $11$ from $x$, which is adding $11$ to $x$.\n\nSo, we add $11$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 11 &= 14 \\\\\nx - 11 + 11 &= 14 + 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 0 &= 25 \\\\\nx &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to the equation is $x = 25$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232040 1065 15042 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232040 1066 #uuid "9a8640a7-fd6f-5576-a1b4-41073b86f03f" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232040 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232041 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we can determine using an equation. By translating a real-world description into an equation, we can solve for the unknown value.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\[math]18$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\[/math]45$, what is the value of $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\[math]18$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 18\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\[/math]45$, we equate this expression to $45$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ns + 18 = 45\n$$\n\nTo isolate $s$, we need to perform the opposite of adding $18$, which is subtracting $18$.\n\nSo, we subtract $18$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 18 &= 45 \\\\\ns + 18 - 18 &= 45 - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 0 &= 27 \\\\\ns &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the service charge is $\\[math]27$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232041 1065 15043 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232041 1066 #uuid "ba992ede-7737-5006-b1da-760889519419" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232041 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232042 1064 "In many real-world situations, we want to describe quantities that must stay *below*, *above*, or *at least* some value.\n\nFor example:\n\n- A budget must stay *below* a certain amount.\n- A factory must produce *at least* a certain number of items.\n- A container must hold *no more than* a certain weight.\n\nSituations like these are modeled using *inequalities*.\n\nFor example, suppose Liam originally had $s$ stickers. He gave $4$ of his stickers to a friend, and now he has fewer than $20$ stickers. Which inequality represents this situation?\n\nWe are told that Liam originally had $s$ stickers.\n\nSince he gave $4$ stickers to a friend, the number of stickers he has now is\n$s - 4$.\n\nBecause Liam now has *fewer than* $20$ stickers, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Liam's stickers after giving some away & < 20 \\\\ s - 4 & < 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n$s - 4 < 20$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232042 1065 15044 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232042 1066 #uuid "d5e17172-9f80-5e0d-991b-6adc9d32755e" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232042 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232043 1064 "Sometimes the variable is not the number being subtracted from, but the number being subtracted.\n\nIn other words, instead of an equation of the form\n$y - 6 = 14$,\nwe may have an equation of the form\n$6 - y = 14$.\n\nIn this case, the variable is the *subtrahend*.\n\nTo isolate the variable, we subtract the constant from both sides of the equation. This creates a negative variable (for example, $-y$), and then we determine the value of the variable by finding the opposite of that number.\n\nFor example, let’s solve for $y$ given that\n\n$$\n6 - y = 14\n$$\n\nTo isolate $y$, we subtract $6$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - y &= 14 \\\\\n6 - y - 6 &= 14 - 6 \\\\\n6 - 6 - y &= 14 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 - y &= 8 \\\\\n- y &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $-y$ (the opposite of $y$) is equal to $8$.\n\nThus, $y$ must be equal to the opposite of $8$, which is $-8$.\n\nTherefore, $y = -8$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232043 1065 15050 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232043 1066 #uuid "c05dc3ce-c587-5624-b6c1-e81bf37fa147" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232043 1067 "One-Step Subtraction Equations With Unknown Subtrahend" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232044 1064 "When a real-world object is shaped like a rectangular prism, we use the volume formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nIf there are several identical objects, first find the volume of one object. Then multiply by the number of objects.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage box is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nlong,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nwide, and $3\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high. Let's find the total volume of $3$ identical boxes.\n\n![](images/7e/7e8defc3f06b1734734e0eb3a2cd34ac4fb4857fa63df0f778f68b07a0f88d72.png)\n\nFirst, find the volume of one box:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{6} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, one box has a volume of $5\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$.\n\nSince there are $3$ boxes,\n\n$$\n3 \\times 5 = 15\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the boxes is $15\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$. Remember to include cubic units when giving a volume." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232044 1065 15052 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232044 1066 #uuid "e1ad0dd1-6932-52f7-b4f1-e5e340ab431b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232044 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232045 1064 "Sometimes we need to find the total cost of a rectangular prism when we know the cost per cubic unit.\n\nTo solve these problems:\n\n1. Find the volume.\n2. Multiply the volume by the cost per cubic unit.\n\nBe sure the units match. Volume is measured in cubic units (such as $\\text{ft}^3$ or $\\text{in}^3$), and the rate is given \"per cubic unit.\"\n\nFor example, suppose a block of soap is shaped like a rectangular prism that is $2\\,\\textrm{in}$ long,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nwide, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nhigh, and each cubic inch of soap costs $\\[math]4$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bef34cddbb058127f9af832bd6ca4e678ae61091820d3ca9e297fa48b3a9f59.png)\n\nTo find the cost of the soap block, we first find the volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{6} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the block has a volume of $4\\,\\text{in}^3$. Since each cubic inch costs $\\[/math]4$, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\[math]4 = \\[/math]16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost of the soap block is $\\[math]16$. Always find the volume first, then multiply by the unit cost." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232045 1065 15056 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232045 1066 #uuid "ca9b17e6-f7ce-5ec1-ad0f-c901145dab68" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232045 1067 "Unit Costs for Volumes" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232046 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we want to determine. By translating the description of the situation into an equation, we can represent the relationship between the quantities.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\[math]15$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\[/math]42$, which equation can be used to find $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\[math]15$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 15\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\[/math]42$, we equate this expression to $42$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\ns + 15 = 42\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232046 1065 15071 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232046 1066 #uuid "11d99b92-6826-5a34-9371-ad393e93f3d4" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232046 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232047 1064 "In a previous lesson, we used $\\max$ and $\\min$ to talk about the largest and smallest *values* a function can take on a set.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn two closely related pieces of notation:\n\n- $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the best (largest/smallest) *function value*.\n- $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the *input value* (the \"argument\") where that best value occurs.\n\nFor example, let\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nand suppose\n\n$$\nf(1)=4,\\qquad f(2)=7,\\qquad f(3)=6\n$$\n\nThen the largest function value is $7$, and it occurs when $x=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= 7, \\\\\n\\arg max_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest function value, and $\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input where that smallest value occurs.\n\n**Watch out!** Sometimes the best value occurs at more than one input. In that case, we need to clarify what $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$) means. We will handle this after we practice the basic case." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232047 1065 15114 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232047 1066 #uuid "bba3a61c-b816-520d-909b-990b65f4c370" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232047 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232048 1064 "Sometimes a function attains its largest (or smallest) value at more than one input. In that case, there are multiple inputs that satisfy the definition of $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$).\n\nIn this course, when a tie occurs, we report the *set* of all inputs where the maximum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\max_{u \\in S} f(u)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when a tie occurs, we report the set of all inputs where the minimum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\min_{u \\in S} f(u)}\n$$\n\nThe quantities $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ are still single numbers." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232048 1065 15115 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232048 1066 #uuid "8ef1fb19-42d3-5471-a53f-023f932c2ad2" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232048 1067 "Argmax and Argmin With Ties" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232049 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned that $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the largest and smallest *function values,* while $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the corresponding *input values.*\n\nIn this lesson, we practice finding $\\max$, $\\arg\\max$, $\\min$, and $\\arg\\min$ from common representations of a function:\n\n- Tables of values\n- Graphs" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232049 1065 15116 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232049 1066 #uuid "a3e684e6-ccf3-5429-8f42-d25353c5ef2d" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232049 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232050 1064 "When a table lists input-output pairs $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in some set $S$, we can read:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by finding the largest output value in the table.\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the input value where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min$, except we look for the smallest output value." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232050 1065 15117 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232050 1066 #uuid "9668d165-c819-5345-ae0d-924ef3496969" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232050 1067 "Finding Argmax and Argmin From a Table" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232051 1064 "When a graph shows the points $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in a set $S$, we can find:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by locating the highest point on the graph and reading its $y$-coordinate.\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the corresponding $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min$, except we look for the lowest point." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232051 1065 15118 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232051 1066 #uuid "874a2ec9-3803-59fe-9157-7b451a113671" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232051 1067 "Finding Argmax and Argmin From a Graph" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232052 1064 "Now, suppose a delivery truck carries $8$ boxes on each trip. If the truck delivers a total of $56$ boxes, which equation can be used to find the number of trips, $t$?\n\nEach trip carries $8$ boxes. If the truck makes $t$ trips, then the total number of boxes delivered can be written as\n\n$$\n8t\n$$\n\nSince the truck delivered a total of $56$ boxes, we equate this expression to $56$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\n8t = 56\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232052 1065 15150 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232052 1066 #uuid "1e614362-fafb-5ce1-beaa-e832e9c2eda6" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232052 1067 "Modeling Using One-Step Multiplication Equations" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232053 1064 "Recall the following length conversions:\n\n- $1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$\n- $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$\n\nWhen we convert from a smaller unit to a larger unit, we may need to use long division to divide by the number of smaller units in one larger unit.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $84$ feet into yards.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between yards and feet:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from feet to yards, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $84$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $84{:}$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84}{3}\n$$\n\nas the division $84 \\div 3$. We can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n|  |  | $\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n84\\,\\textrm{ft} = 28\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use long division to simplify the quotients that appear when converting measurements to larger units." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232053 1065 15192 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232053 1066 #uuid "acaf1894-f7cb-5d8c-b88d-97fb846d6f5b" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232053 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232054 1064 "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232054 1065 15195 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232054 1066 #uuid "48e375bd-70b2-58b8-9f53-dd21756f2f20" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232054 1067 "Two-Step Length Conversions" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232055 1064 "The **surface area** of a polyhedron is the combined area of all its faces.\n\nTo calculate the surface area of a polyhedron, we use nets.\n\nLet's consider the net of the rectangular pyramid below. What is the surface area of this pyramid?\n\n![](images/1b/1b8db0cb92f9fe3087483e5b0c7077f23a5037ac7f890ecce548b4ce0c4e27ff.png)\n\nThe surface area of our pyramid consists of one rectangle (the base) and four triangles (the side faces).\n\nAlso, notice that there are two different triangular faces:\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $60\\,\\text{in}^2$, and\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $30\\,\\text{in}^2$.\n\nCalculating the total area, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot 120in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 60in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 30in^{2} \\\\\n&= 120in^{2} + 120in^{2} + 60in^{2} \\\\\n&= 300in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the surface area of the pyramid is $300 \\, \\text{in}^2$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232055 1065 15200 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232055 1066 #uuid "c253e5c0-62a1-5d0a-9200-f738cdded990" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232055 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232056 1064 "If we know a polyhedron's measurements, we can calculate its surface area by first drawing the net of the polyhedron. Then, we calculate the areas of its polygonal faces using known formulas and then sum the results.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the surface area of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93d93a9a0dff25bb55a411ba155388f3769487710f2a8d847b0545b113655a02.png)\n\nFirst, we consider the net of our prism.\n\n![](images/aa/aa0cccf3b41ccdfd946f6df6ec3c41eb8ee4c6dbd469b5ca36dd6b5c50d5858b.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the prism is made up of squares and rectangles.\n\n- The rectangles are $8\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n$\\text{Area of rectangle} = 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n- The squares have sides of length $2\\, \\text{ft}$. So, the area of one square is\n$\\text{Area of square} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n\nFinally, the surface area of the prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 4 \\cdot (Area of rectangle) + 2 \\cdot (Area of square) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 16 + 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 64 + 8 \\\\\n&= 72ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232056 1065 15201 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232056 1066 #uuid "f631a93e-6959-5b54-922f-0bc5f41c5ccb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232056 1067 "Finding Surface Areas of Polyhedra Given Some Lengths" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232057 1064 "The **perimeter** of a polygon is the total distance around its boundary. So, to find the perimeter, we add the lengths of all the polygon's sides.\n\nFor example, let's find the perimeter of the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03371bcd082bbda3488418ec0af5b7ffded11b0ce2dd6837c8522c8f1a593c5c.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the triangle is\n\n$$\nP = 12 + 9 + 11 = 32 \\: \\text{yd}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232057 1065 15326 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232057 1066 #uuid "63684ac0-bf22-5757-a0a1-508ae6f03a20" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232057 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232058 1064 "Now, let's see how to find the perimeter of the rectangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b3b8005a3d2f9294df3048acd0d6fb7186445de56381e5aafc4dacd200cc90.png)\n\nTo find the perimeter, we add the lengths of all the rectangle's sides.\n\nRecall that a rectangle is a quadrilateral with two pairs of equal parallel sides and four right angles.\n\n![](images/77/7734f24982b6147bad1a70d5d7eaec8884fdbc18a2c562a05212cb309b1ff461.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 2 \\cdot (Length + Height) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (9 + 4) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 13 \\\\\n&= 26in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232058 1065 15327 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232058 1066 #uuid "e0b12eac-b5ce-5db9-a145-d8c173405f02" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232058 1067 "Perimeters of Rectangles" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232059 1064 "Recall the following properties of rhombuses and squares:\n\n- A square is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, where *all four sides are equal*, and all four angles are right angles.![](images/9d/9d7f01ef9e4c3bdb2d87c361c6e1aca37be00ffc0be81e446433cf0a9f5e88ff.png)\n- A rhombus is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, where *all four sides are equal*.![](images/55/5584f312d74c98928eb3f368acc89dcbdb2ddf2adab82db75f7b7fb842377a1b.png)\n\nThe special properties of these shapes allow us to find their perimeters even more easily than usual.\n\nFor example, the perimeter of a square with a side of length $7\\:\\text{m}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 4 \\cdot (Side Length) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 28m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232059 1065 15328 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232059 1066 #uuid "eabf7f6b-8f62-5956-a69a-7041fa67e50c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232059 1067 "Perimeters of Rhombuses and Squares" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232060 1064 "Now, suppose $h=35$. Let's compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}7\n$$\n\nAs before, replacing $h$ with $35$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h}{7} &=  \\\\\nh \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n35 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 5 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232060 1065 15343 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232060 1066 #uuid "af128711-923c-57c7-8fea-13f16a069b13" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232060 1067 "Expressions With Multiplication and Division" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232061 1064 "We can combine several arithmetic operations in linear expressions.\n\nFor example, given that $m = 6$, let's compute $2m + 5$.\n\nReplacing $m$ with $6$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2m + 5 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 6 + 5 &=  \\\\\n12 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 17 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232061 1065 15344 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232061 1066 #uuid "43648158-2cb2-5944-8113-008f48a7434c" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232061 1067 "Combining Different Operations in Expressions" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232062 1064 "Finally, let's see how we can evaluate linear expressions in context.\n\nSuppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) after $t$ hours of a liquid mixture as it cools is given by the expression\n$38 - 4t$.\nWhat is the temperature of the mixture after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours?\n\nTo determine the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours, we replace $t$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n38 - 4t &=  \\\\\n38 - 4 \\cdot t &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} &=  \\\\\n38 - 2 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n38 - 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours is $28\\,^\\circ\\textrm{C}$." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232062 1065 15345 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232062 1066 #uuid "24a55d2e-53c7-5fd7-9c6e-d8522f61dca4" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232062 1067 "Evaluating Linear Expressions in Context" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232063 1064 "With the help of a number line, we can place a set of positive and negative whole numbers in order.\nLet's place the following set of numbers from *least* to *greatest*:\n$-3, \\; 1, \\; 2, \\; -1, \\; 4$\n\nFirst, we plot all of these numbers on a number line:\n\n![](images/18/18a86d35ed43e284ca2a9f62e314e253c77128c3ea14cc9e579fdd467da9aefd.png)\n\nRecall that numbers to the left of a value on the line are less than the value.\nSo, going from left to right, we have:\n$-3< -1 < 1 < 2 < 4$\n\nFinally, we can write the numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n-3, \\; -1, \\; 1, \\; 2, \\; 4\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232063 1065 2098 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232063 1066 #uuid "312d084b-5d61-58c1-b66b-14e32b6029fe" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232063 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232064 1064 "We can use our knowledge of ordering whole numbers to place rational numbers in order.\nTo do this, we write the numbers with a common denominator, and then compare the numerators.\n\nFor example, let's place the following set of numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}, \\;-3, \\; 1, \\;4\n$$\n\nFirst, we write all of the numbers as fractions with a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; -\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; \\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}2}, \\; \\dfrac {8}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNext, we bring any negative signs inside the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}, \\; \\dfrac{(-6)}{2}, \\; \\dfrac{2}{2}, \\; \\dfrac 8 2\n$$\n\nNow, we order the numerators *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-6)}{2} < \\dfrac{2}{2} < \\dfrac 5 2 < \\dfrac{8}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we change each fraction back to its original form:\n\n$$\n-3 < 1 < \\dfrac{5}{2} < 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the numbers in order, from *least* to *greatest*:\n\n$$\n-3, \\; 1, \\; \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\; 4\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232064 1065 2100 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232064 1066 #uuid "1e5c6b66-1b22-51b2-899f-27be9a44b4bb" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232064 1067 "Ordering Rational Numbers" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232065 1064 "A negative number often means a measure in the *opposite direction*. The magnitude of the measure is given by the *absolute value* of the number.\n\nFor instance, suppose a fish is at a position of $-10\\,\\textrm{m}$ relative to sea level.\n\n- The negative sign tells us the direction is *below sea level*, which is the opposite of *above sea level*.\n- To find the actual distance the fish is below sea level (its *depth*), we take the absolute value of its position:\n$\\mid -10 \\mid = 10$\n\nTherefore, the fish is $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ below sea level." 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232065 1065 2113 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232065 1066 #uuid "ac87f642-7c21-558a-af4d-e8ee8c6d5a70" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232065 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232066 1064 "We can use vertical number lines, similar to thermometers, to represent measurements that have a *zero point*.\n\nFor instance, *sea level* is a common zero point for measuring altitude and depth.\n\n- Locations *above* sea level have positive values.\n- Locations *below* sea level have negative values.\n\nSuppose a scientist finds a shipwreck $100 \\text{m}$ **below sea level**. We can represent this depth as $-100 \\text{m}$.\n\nThe diagram below shows the location of the shipwreck.\n\n![](images/cc/cc361bd9ec12c09e256105cef4f918832d8ff1cbb707d35e9285befbd3c1f18f.png)" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232066 1065 2114 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232066 1066 #uuid "ba520ac2-00f5-5802-968e-a137f012421a" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232066 1067 "Using Scales for More Measures" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232067 1064 "Another way to model fractions is with a point on a number line from $0$ to $1$.\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the denominator is the total number of equal parts that the line is split into, and\n- the numerator is the number of steps to the point from $0$ (left to right).\n\nFor example, let's find the fraction represented by the number line below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf2df9409acfb58acd879fb3dfc3f3579a79fd102c6258da246a1cb9226b78e.png)\n\nNotice that the line is split into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. And the number that we are interested in is located $\\color{red}2$ steps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/05/05d4093f318810dd2086c2c6166219890a3f557707b89c323ce82cb843854d87.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232067 1066 #uuid "21371798-671a-5c3a-b224-fa552b4b5aa9" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232067 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232068 1064 "We can represent mixed numbers with number lines.\n\nFor example, let's write the number shown on the number line below as a mixed number.\n\n![](images/f4/f4e1b05632d359a21c097d114afd51d9005e942c6badcf89871b312d2a3e958f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/77/77ef5e27b471e072fd3e3be72447325b2b6b49081075f9d52b094438a0345833.png)\n\nWe can land on our point by making $\\color{red}1$ whole step plus $\\color{blue}2$ part steps (out of a possible ${\\color{blue}{3}})$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9d9500d6b8f1fcc000a6c4e06449f789b92c30ed25469862b01e1929e770397.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232068 1066 #uuid "ef139b75-3216-563d-bcd7-37e8a1139b41" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true] [17592186232068 1067 "Introduction" 13194139533382 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"__map/00000000000000000000" 17592186230251 "__map/00000000000000000001" 17592186230252 "__map/00000000000000000002" 17592186230253 "__map/00000000000000000003" 17592186230254 "__map/00000000000000000004" 17592186230255 "__map/00000000000000000005" 17592186230256 "__map/00000000000000000006" 17592186230257 "__map/00000000000000000007" 17592186230258 "__map/00000000000000000008" 17592186230259 "__map/00000000000000000009" 17592186230260 "__map/00000000000000000010" 17592186230261 "__map/00000000000000000011" 17592186230262 "__map/00000000000000000012" 17592186230263 "__map/00000000000000000013" 17592186230264 "__map/00000000000000000014" 17592186230265 "__map/00000